// Chapter 8, "Human Factors." Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-11):
// Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge, FAA-H-8083-25C: https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-25c.pdf
//   chapter 2: the six ADM steps ("good judgment can be taught"); risk management's four principles (accept no unnecessary risk; make risk
//   decisions at the appropriate level, never ATC or passengers; accept risk when benefits outweigh dangers; integrate risk management into
//   planning at all levels); SRM definition (ADM, RM, TM, AM, CFIT awareness, SA); hazard versus risk; Figure 2-4, the five hazardous attitudes
//   and antidotes, quoted verbatim: anti-authority "Don't tell me." / "Follow the rules. They are usually right."; impulsivity "Do it quickly."
//   / "Not so fast. Think first."; invulnerability "It won't happen to me." / "It could happen to me."; macho "I can do it." / "Taking chances
//   is foolish."; resignation "What's the use?" / "I'm not helpless. I can make a difference."; label the thought hazardous, then state the
//   antidote; women equally susceptible to macho; IMSAFE (Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol "within 8 hours? Within 24 hours?", Fatigue,
//   Emotion); legal versus smart; PAVE (Pilot in command, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures; survival gear and clothing over remote
//   areas); "Management of external pressure is the single most important key to risk management"; personal SOPs; 3P (Perceive, Process,
//   Perform) with CARE (Consequences, Alternatives, Reality, External factors) and TEAM (Transfer, Eliminate, Accept, Mitigate); "if you find
//   yourself saying that it will 'probably' be okay, it is definitely time for a solid reality check"; DECIDE (Detect, Estimate, Choose a
//   course of action, Identify solutions, Do the necessary actions, Evaluate the effects); Figure 2-15 operational pitfalls; Figure 2-16
//   psychological stressors including "a death in the family."
//   chapter 17: hypoxic, hypemic (CO the most common form; blood donation), stagnant, and histotoxic hypoxia (one ounce of alcohol about 2,000
//   feet of physiological altitude); symptoms with euphoria first; Figure 17-1 time of useful consciousness (20,000 ft 30 minutes or more;
//   22,000 5 to 10 minutes; 25,000 3 to 5; 28,000 2.5 to 3; 30,000 1 to 2; 35,000 30 to 60 seconds; 40,000 15 to 20; 45,000 9 to 15);
//   susceptibility regardless of endurance or acclimatization; hyperventilation symptoms and treatment (slow the breathing rate, a paper bag,
//   talking aloud); middle ear (Valsalva), sinus block, decongestants; spatial disorientation (vestibular, somatosensory, visual; 2 degrees
//   per second threshold; the leans, Coriolis, graveyard spiral, somatogravic, inversion, elevator, false horizon, autokinesis; demonstrations
//   not at low altitude and only with an instructor; coping list); landing illusions (runway width, slope, featureless terrain, water
//   refraction, haze and clear high altitude air, fog, ground lighting); motion sickness (Dramamine not recommended for pilots); CO (about 200
//   times more easily than oxygen, up to 48 hours to clear, heater and defrost vents, detectors change color; heater off, vents and windows
//   open, oxygen; smoking like 8,000 feet); stress acute and chronic; fatigue acute, skill, chronic ("If suffering from acute fatigue, stay on
//   the ground"); dehydration (2 to 4 quarts per 24 hours, thirst at about a 1.5 quart deficit or 2 percent of body weight); Figure 17-8
//   drink table (12 oz light beer and 4 oz table wine both .48 oz alcohol); about 3 hours to clear one drink; hangover is still under the
//   influence; over 16 hours; altitude multiplies alcohol; 61.53 and 91.17 summaries; diphenhydramine named; marijuana "is an illicit drug per
//   federal law and its use by airmen is prohibited"; stimulants; DCS after scuba.
// 14 CFR 91.17, 91.211, 61.53, read through the eCFR versioner API, Title 14 as of 2026-10-01:
//   https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-10-01/title-14.xml?part=91&section=91.17 (same call for 91.211 and part=61&section=61.53).
//   91.17(a)(1) to (4) quoted: within 8 hours after the consumption of any alcoholic beverage; while under the influence of alcohol; while
//   using any drug that affects the person's faculties in any way contrary to safety; alcohol concentration of 0.04 or greater. (b) no
//   intoxicated person carried except a medical patient under proper care. (c) test on request of an authorized officer; results of tests
//   within 4 hours. 91.211(a)(1) above 12,500 up to and including 14,000 cabin pressure altitude, required minimum flight crew provided with
//   and uses oxygen for the part of the flight at those altitudes of more than 30 minutes; (a)(2) above 14,000 the entire flight time at
//   those altitudes; (a)(3) above 15,000 each occupant provided with supplemental oxygen. 61.53(a)(1) and (2) quoted.
// AIM chapter 8, section 1, 8-1-1 through 8-1-5: https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap8_section_1.html
//   (safest rule, no flying with any illness or any medication unless FAA approved; 12 to 24 hours bottle to throttle; fatigue causes include
//   "strong emotional pressure"; Emotion: "death of a family member ... should not fly until satisfactorily recovered"; I'M SAFE personal
//   checklist; night vision deteriorates at a cabin altitude as low as 5,000 ft; significant hypoxia effects usually not below 12,000 in a
//   healthy pilot; 15 minutes at 15,000; oxygen recommended above 10,000 by day and 5,000 at night; ear and sinus block; decongestant sprays
//   usually inadequate; scuba waits of 12 and 24 hours up to 8,000 ft and 24 hours above; hyperventilation; CO from manifold heaters; the
//   Coriolis illusion "most overwhelming of all illusions in flight").
// Risk Management Handbook, FAA-H-8083-2A: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf
//   chapter 2 (six steps for personal minimums; flight categories; Figure 2-9 adjustments: add at least 500 ft to ceiling, 1/2 mile to
//   visibility, 500 ft to runway length, subtract at least 5 knots from winds, adjust for each factor; cautions: don't adjust to complete a
//   specific flight, one variable at a time, consult a CFI; a written set helps explain decisions to passengers) and chapter 6 (expectation
//   bias, including perceiving the usual taxi clearance; confirmation bias; fatigue makes both worse; emotional shock).
// Private Pilot Airplane ACS, FAA-S-ACS-6C, PA.I.H (K1a to K1l including hypothermia and scuba, K2, K3, K4, R1 to R4, S1) and PA.VIII:
//   https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/acs/private_airplane_acs_6.pdf
// Airplane Flying Handbook, FAA-H-8083-3C, chapter 18, inadvertent VFR into IMC (trust the instruments; keep the wings level; fingertip
//   control; no more than 10 degrees of bank; searching outside while half on instruments leads to loss of control):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/19_afh_ch18.pdf
// Instrument Flying Handbook, FAA-H-8083-15B, nose-low unusual attitude recovery (reduce power, level the wings with coordinated aileron and
//   rudder, raise the nose with smooth back pressure; the instinct is to pull):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/FAA-H-8083-15B.pdf
// Course canon: KAPA 5,885 ft; Bennett about 5,506 ft; Denver Class B floor 8,000 MSL over KAPA and the practice area; Abby enters at 7,700,
//   300 under the floor, never higher. Runway 10/28 is 4,800 ft. Jordan works 24 on, 48 off, 7 AM to 7 AM (shift starts in this stretch: Oct
//   21, 24, 27, 30, Nov 2, then Nov 5 traded away to a shift in mid November; Nov 8).
// Invented for the story: Danny's birthday, November 5 (born 1984, he'd have been 42; he died in March 2026 at 41); the $410 monthly share
//   cost; every METAR, TAF, ATIS, NOTAM, and runway closure; the fictional safety meeting case; all people's actions. The carboxyhemoglobin
//   reading on the station monitor is story detail from general clinical knowledge, not from an FAA source. The 2026 time change is Sunday,
//   November 1. Dates: Thu Oct 22, Fri Oct 23, Thu Oct 29, Sat Oct 31, Sun Nov 1, Wed Nov 4, Thu Nov 5, Fri Nov 6, Sat Nov 7.
//   MDT = UTC minus 6 through Oct 31; MST = UTC minus 7 from Nov 1.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 8, part: `Part One: The Share`, title: `Human Factors`, weeks: `October to November`,
  tasks: [`I.H`,`VIII`,`IX`,`III.A`],
  opening: [
    `The debrief was Thursday at four in the briefing room, two days after the shirt tail went up on the corkboard, and Abby had already written it on the whiteboard in green before Jordan got there. SOLO OCT 20. WHAT WENT RIGHT. WHAT YOU'D WANT BACK. WHAT'S NEXT.`,
    `They did the first two columns in forty minutes. The door. The float on the second approach. The go-around, which Abby circled. Jordan had come straight from sleeping off a shift that had ended at seven, and she kept her coffee in both hands and answered everything in order, the way she'd give a report at the ER doors. Abby wrote it all down and didn't say good once, which Jordan had learned meant it was.`,
    `Under WHAT'S NEXT there were three lines. <b>1. Solo laps until I'm bored. 2. Hood work and the practice area. 3. You.</b>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Me."</span>`,
    `Abby slid a stapled packet across the table. The cover said RISK MANAGEMENT HANDBOOK, CHAPTER 2, PERSONAL MINIMUMS, and under that, in Abby's capitals, FILL IN EVERY BOX. NOT MY NUMBERS. YOURS. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Next chapter in the book is the pilot. It's the part of the airplane that fails the most, and it doesn't have a squawk list. I don't sign you for the practice area alone until that's done."</span>`,
    `Jordan put it in her flight bag, next to the green notebook. It stayed there for two weeks.`,
    `Afterward she went out to the ramp and sat in Biscuit for a minute with the door open, the way she'd started doing, not for any reason. The low sun came in across the panel. Above the mag switch, above Ellie's HI DAD sticker with its corners curling, there was a rectangle of paint a shade darker than the rest of the panel, a little bigger than a playing card, with two old corners of yellowed tape still stuck to it. She'd seen it in April and filed it the way you file a scar on a patient: something used to be there. She looked at it longer this time. She still didn't ask anybody.`,
    `At 8:14 that night her mother texted.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Lourdes Reyes" time="8:14 PM">Thursday the 5th is your brother's birthday. Pozole at mine at 12. Bring back my good bowls. Claire will come after work.</prop>`,
    `She'd traded that shift away in September, the first trade she'd made all year, and hadn't told anybody why. She put the lunch in her phone. Then she sat looking at the date for a while. He'd have been forty two.`,
    `The club chat, meanwhile, had found the weather.`,
    `<prop type="chat" from="Marisol Tran" time="9:02 PM">First cold week coming. Biscuit's cabin heat is a shroud around the muffler. If you pull the knob all the way out, open a vent too. Look at the CO card by the compass when you do. I mean it.</prop>`,
    `<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="9:05 PM">marisol is the only person alive who can make heat sound threatening</prop>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: the part of the airplane that's you`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The part that fails most`,
        body: [`Sit. We've spent six months on the airplane: the engine, the weather, the air, the box around the runway. Today is the part that fails most often, and it's the one sitting in your chair. The handbook calls it human factors and doesn't soften it. Most accidents trace back to a decision somebody made. Usually a reasonable sounding one, made a little tired, a little late, by a person who wanted the flight to happen.`,
          `The FAA's name for doing this on purpose is aeronautical decision making, ADM. The PHAK makes a claim I like: good judgment can be taught. The old idea was that judgment grows by itself as you log hours. It doesn't, or not reliably. ADM is a set of habits. Spot the attitudes that hurt pilots. Learn to change the behavior. Learn to recognize stress and cope with it. Build risk assessment skills. Use every resource you've got. Then grade yourself afterward on how you decided, and not only on how it turned out.`,
          `Two words the test treats carefully. A hazard is the thing itself: a condition, an event, a circumstance. A nick in the prop. Gusts on the ATIS. Four hours of sleep. Risk is your assessment of what that hazard could do to you, how likely and how bad. Two pilots can look at the same hazard and see different risk, which is why this chapter exists.`,
          `Risk management has four principles, and I want them in this order. Accept no unnecessary risk. Make risk decisions at the appropriate level, which means the pilot in command, and the handbook says never let anyone else make them for you, not ATC and not your passengers. Accept risk when the benefits outweigh the costs. And build risk management into planning at every level, in the air too, not only at the kitchen table.`,
          `Over all of it the handbook puts single-pilot resource management, SRM: the art and science of managing all the resources available to a single pilot, on board and outside, before and during the flight. It bundles ADM, risk management, task management, automation management, CFIT awareness (controlled flight into terrain, which is flying a perfectly good airplane into the ground), and situational awareness. Alone, there's nobody in the right seat to catch you. SRM is what you use instead. ATC is a resource. A passenger who's never flown is a resource; they can look for traffic. Your checklist is a resource. So is calling it off.`],
        terms: [[`Hazard`,`A real or perceived condition, event, or circumstance a pilot encounters.`],[`Risk`,`The pilot's assessment of a hazard's possible impact: how likely, how severe.`],[`ADM`,`Aeronautical decision making. A systematic approach to analyzing changes in flight and choosing a safe course. Good judgment can be taught.`],[`SRM`,`Single-pilot resource management: managing all resources, on board and outside, before and during flight. Includes ADM, RM, TM, AM, CFIT awareness, and SA.`],[`Four principles`,`Accept no unnecessary risk. Decide at the appropriate level. Accept risk when benefits outweigh costs. Integrate risk management at all levels.`]],
        exam: `Know hazard versus risk, the four risk management principles, and the pieces of SRM. "Never let ATC or passengers make risk decisions for you" is straight from the handbook.` },
      { h: `IMSAFE`,
        body: [`Since the discovery flight I've asked you the same thing at the wing. Sleep, meds, sick, anything. That's IMSAFE with the letters filed off. The AIM calls it a personal checklist and says to run it on yourself the way you run one on the airplane, because pilot impairment contributes to many more accidents than failures of aircraft systems.`,
          `Illness: am I sick? The AIM's safest rule is not to fly with any illness. A cold you'd work through on the ambulance is a cold that blocks your ears on the way down. Medication: am I taking anything that might affect my judgment or make me drowsy? Stress: pressure from the job, money, health, family. The handbook points out that the regulations list conditions that ground you and stress isn't one, so nobody's going to count it for you. Alcohol: have I been drinking within 8 hours? Within 24? Fatigue: am I tired and not adequately rested? Emotion: am I emotionally upset?`,
          `The AIM's paragraph on emotion is short. I'll read it once and then leave it alone. Certain emotionally upsetting events, including a serious argument, death of a family member, separation or divorce, loss of job, and financial catastrophe, can render a pilot unable to fly an aircraft safely. Any pilot who experiences an emotionally upsetting event should not fly until satisfactorily recovered from it. The PHAK lists a death in the family by name under psychological stress. So grief is on the checklist. It sits there the way icing sits in a weather briefing. It's real, and it's hard to see from the inside.`,
          `There's a regulation behind part of this. 61.53 says a person who holds a medical certificate may not act as pilot in command while they know or have reason to know of any medical condition that would make them unable to meet the requirements for that medical, or while they're taking medication or getting other treatment for a condition that leaves them unable to meet those requirements. Your medical says you were fit the day the doctor saw you. 61.53 makes you the one who checks it again on the day you fly.`,
          `The handbook gets specific about pills. Over the counter antihistamines and decongestants can cause drowsiness and fuzzy thinking. Diphenhydramine, Benadryl, gets called out by name, because it makes you drowsy and stays in your system a long time. The AIM's safest rule is not to fly while taking any medication unless the FAA has approved it. And remember that the condition you're treating can ground you by itself. The pill makes you feel better. It doesn't make the cold go away.`],
        terms: [[`IMSAFE`,`Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue, Emotion. A personal checklist run before every flight.`],[`AIM, Emotion`,`Emotionally upsetting events, including the death of a family member, can make a pilot unable to fly safely. Don't fly until satisfactorily recovered.`],[`61.53(a)`,`No PIC while knowing of a medical condition, or taking medication or treatment for one, that would make you unable to meet your medical certificate's requirements.`],[`Diphenhydramine`,`An OTC antihistamine the PHAK names: drowsiness and a long half-life.`]],
        exam: `Expect IMSAFE letter by letter, 61.53's two prohibitions, and the point that stress isn't a regulatory grounding condition but still has to be counted.` },
      { h: `Bottle to throttle`,
        body: [`91.17 is four lines and the test loves all of them. No person may act or attempt to act as a crewmember of a civil aircraft within 8 hours after the consumption of any alcoholic beverage, while under the influence of alcohol, while using any drug that affects the person's faculties in any way contrary to safety, or while having an alcohol concentration of 0.04 or greater in a blood or breath specimen. Those are four separate rules. Ten hours after your last beer with a 0.05, you're grounded. Seven hours after one beer with a 0.00, you're grounded. And the PHAK says it in plain words: while experiencing a hangover, a pilot is still under the influence of alcohol.`,
          `The same section says you can't let a person who appears intoxicated ride in your airplane, except in an emergency or a medical patient under proper care. You know what that looks like better than I do. If an authorized law enforcement officer asks you to take a test, you take it. And the FAA can ask for the results of any test taken within 4 hours after you flew.`,
          `Now the numbers that aren't regulations. A 12 ounce light beer, a 4 ounce glass of table wine, and a shot of liquor all carry about half an ounce of alcohol. The handbook says your body needs about 3 hours to get rid of one drink, and that considerable amounts can stay in you for over 16 hours. The AIM calls 8 hours the legal line and then says an excellent rule is 12 to 24 hours between bottle and throttle, depending on how much. Altitude makes it worse. Alcohol keeps the brain from using oxygen, a kind of histotoxic hypoxia, and the PHAK says one ounce of alcohol can equal about 2,000 extra feet of physiological altitude. You live at 5,885 feet. Do that math before the second beer.`,
          `Drugs. The FAA's position on marijuana is in the handbook in quotes: marijuana is an illicit drug per federal law and its use by airmen is prohibited. The dispensary down the road doesn't change that. You hold a federal certificate. Stimulants count too: caffeine, nicotine, the energy drink at hour twenty of a shift. They're drugs, and their main trick is hiding fatigue from you.`],
        terms: [[`91.17(a)`,`No acting as crewmember within 8 hours of any alcohol, while under the influence, while using a drug that affects faculties contrary to safety, or at 0.04 or greater.`],[`Hangover`,`PHAK: still under the influence of alcohol.`],[`AIM bottle to throttle`,`An excellent rule is at least 12 to 24 hours, depending on the amount.`],[`Alcohol and altitude`,`Histotoxic hypoxia. One ounce of alcohol can add about 2,000 ft of physiological altitude.`]],
        exam: `The four parts of 91.17 are separate tests and you have to pass all four. 8 hours and 0.04 are the numbers. The AIM's 12 to 24 hours is a recommendation, not the rule.` },
      { h: `Tired, thirsty, hungry, cold`,
        body: [`Fatigue goes at the top of your list, because of your job. The handbook says fatigue is frequently associated with pilot error, and the AIM says it may not be apparent to a pilot until serious errors are made. You have a long history of being good at things while exhausted. Alone in a cockpit, that history is a hazard.`,
          `Two kinds. Acute fatigue is short term: a long day, hard work, excitement, a night without sleep. The AIM's list of causes includes heavy mental workload and strong emotional pressure, which I'd like you to notice. Rest and 8 hours of sound sleep usually fix it. A special kind is skill fatigue. Your timing goes a little off, so a landing turns into separate pieces instead of one motion, and your attention shrinks to the middle of your vision. That's when the airspeed you aren't looking at wanders off. Chronic fatigue is when there's not enough recovery between the acute ones, over weeks or months. It usually has psychological roots, rest alone doesn't cure it, and the handbook says see a physician.`,
          `The rule is one sentence. If you're suffering from acute fatigue, stay on the ground. The handbook says no amount of training or experience overcomes it once it's in the flight deck with you, and it says to avoid flying without a full night's rest, after working excessive hours, or after an especially exhausting or stressful day. You've made that call once already, in a parking lot at 7:25 in the morning. Make it every time.`,
          `Dehydration sits right next to it. Denver is dry, Biscuit's cabin heat is drier, and coffee is a diuretic. The first noticeable effect of dehydration, the handbook says, is fatigue. It suggests two to four quarts of water every 24 hours, and points out that thirst shows up late, around a quart and a half down or 2 percent of your body weight. Carry a bottle. Drink before you want it. And eat. Missed meals and low blood sugar are on the handbook's list of physiological stressors, and a granola bar standing up in the station kitchen is a snack, at best.`,
          `The ACS also lists hypothermia, which out here means dressing for where you might land instead of where you took off. A wheat field east of Bennett in November is a cold place to wait for somebody to find you. The PAVE checklist asks whether you have the clothing, water, and survival gear for the route. In a Cessna with a heater that works some of the time, the answer is a real coat.`],
        terms: [[`Acute fatigue`,`Short term. Causes include sleep loss, strain, heavy mental workload, and strong emotional pressure. Fixed by rest and sleep.`],[`Skill fatigue`,`Timing disruption and a narrowed perceptual field. Smooth control turns into separate pieces.`],[`Chronic fatigue`,`Long term, not relieved by rest alone, usually psychological roots. See a physician.`],[`Dehydration`,`First effect is fatigue. Drink 2 to 4 quarts per 24 hours. Thirst arrives late.`]],
        exam: `"If suffering from acute fatigue, stay on the ground." Know acute versus chronic, and that fatigue is often invisible to the tired pilot.` },
      { h: `Thin air`,
        body: [`Hypoxia means not enough oxygen, and the brain is the first organ that minds. The handbook sorts it four ways by cause. Hypoxic hypoxia is not enough getting in. At altitude the air is still about 21 percent oxygen, but the pressure is lower, so fewer molecules make it across in your lungs. Hypemic hypoxia is blood that can't carry what's there: anemia, bleeding, a recent blood donation, and most often carbon monoxide parked on your hemoglobin. Stagnant hypoxia is blood that isn't moving: shock, a heart not pumping well, high G loads, cold. Histotoxic hypoxia is cells that can't use the oxygen they're given: alcohol, narcotics, poisons.`,
          `The first symptoms can be euphoria and a carefree feeling, which is the meanest line in the book. You feel great right up to the part where you can't add. Then headache, slower reactions, impaired judgment, visual impairment, drowsiness, dizziness, tingling, numbness, and cyanosis, blue lips and fingernails. Your field of vision narrows. The AIM says a normal healthy pilot usually doesn't see significant effects below 12,000 feet, that from 12,000 to 15,000 judgment, memory, alertness, and coordination go, and that performance can seriously deteriorate within 15 minutes at 15,000. One detail for us: night vision starts to deteriorate at a cabin altitude as low as 5,000 feet. Centennial is 5,885. Every night flight you ever take here starts above that line.`,
          `Time of useful consciousness is how long you have to make rational, lifesaving decisions and carry them out at a given altitude without supplemental oxygen. The handbook's table: 30 minutes or more at 20,000 feet, 5 to 10 minutes at 22,000, 3 to 5 at 25,000, 1 to 2 at 30,000, and 9 to 15 seconds at 45,000. Biscuit will never see 20,000. Look at the shape of the curve. It falls off a cliff.`,
          `Then the regulation, 91.211. At cabin pressure altitudes above 12,500 feet MSL up to and including 14,000, the required minimum flight crew has to be provided with and use supplemental oxygen for the part of the flight at those altitudes that's more than 30 minutes long. Above 14,000, the crew uses it the entire time. Above 15,000, each occupant has to be provided with supplemental oxygen. Provided. The passengers don't have to wear it. The test likes that word. In an unpressurized airplane, cabin altitude is the altitude you're flying.`,
          `The AIM goes further than the rule and recommends oxygen above 10,000 feet in the day and above 5,000 at night. Living in Denver doesn't buy you anything. The handbook says all pilots are susceptible, regardless of physical endurance or acclimatization. Treatment is lower altitude, supplemental oxygen, or both.`],
        terms: [[`Hypoxic hypoxia`,`Not enough oxygen available to the body, as at altitude where partial pressure is low.`],[`Hypemic hypoxia`,`Blood can't carry enough oxygen. Most common cause: carbon monoxide.`],[`Stagnant hypoxia`,`Blood isn't flowing to the tissues: shock, poor circulation, G loads, cold.`],[`Histotoxic hypoxia`,`Cells can't use oxygen: alcohol, narcotics, poisons.`],[`91.211(a)`,`Crew uses O2 after 30 minutes above 12,500 to 14,000; the entire time above 14,000; every occupant provided O2 above 15,000.`]],
        exam: `Match the hypoxia type to its cause (CO is hypemic, alcohol is histotoxic). Know the three altitude bands in 91.211 and the word "provided" above 15,000.` },
      { h: `Breathing, ears, and stomachs`,
        body: [`Hyperventilation is the opposite problem: breathing too fast and too deep, so you blow off too much carbon dioxide. It happens more than people think, usually when something scares you. Symptoms overlap with hypoxia: lightheaded, dizzy, tingling, hot and cold, muscle spasms, visual impairment, and at the end, unconsciousness. The fix is to slow your breathing on purpose. The handbook says talking out loud helps, and a paper bag works. You talk to yourself in the pattern. I've noticed. Keep doing it. If you're ever on oxygen when symptoms start, the AIM says go to 100 percent oxygen and check the system first, because the two can happen at the same time.`,
          `Ears. Your middle ear connects to your throat through the Eustachian tube. Going up, the air inside expands and leaks out on its own. Coming down, the outside pressure rises and you have to open the tube yourself: swallow, yawn, or close your mouth, pinch your nose, and blow gently, the Valsalva maneuver. With a cold the tube may not open at all. That's an ear block: severe pain, hearing loss for hours or days, sometimes a ruptured eardrum. A sinus block is the same thing in your sinuses, worst on descent, sometimes bad enough to make your upper teeth ache. The AIM's prevention is the same for both: don't fly with an upper respiratory infection or a nasal allergy. It also says decongestant sprays and drops usually aren't adequate protection, and that oral decongestants have side effects that can impair you. So the cure for a stuffy nose is a day off.`,
          `Motion sickness is your brain getting messages from your eyes and your inner ear that don't agree. Plenty of students get it in the first few lessons and it fades. Anxiety makes it worse. Open the fresh air vents, look outside at the horizon, keep your head still, and tell me. It's no reflection on you as a pilot. Dramamine helps passengers. The handbook doesn't recommend it for pilots, because it makes you drowsy.`,
          `One more the ACS lists: nitrogen after scuba diving. The AIM wants at least 12 hours before flying up to 8,000 feet after diving that didn't need decompression stops, and at least 24 hours after diving that did. Above 8,000 feet, at least 24 hours after any dive. The nitrogen you soaked up at depth comes out of solution when the pressure drops, and it comes out in your joints.`],
        terms: [[`Hyperventilation`,`Excessive breathing that blows off CO2. Treat by slowing the breathing rate, talking aloud, or breathing into a bag.`],[`Ear block`,`Eustachian tube can't equalize, usually on descent. Don't fly with a cold or nasal allergy.`],[`Valsalva maneuver`,`Mouth closed, nose pinched, blow gently to open the Eustachian tubes.`],[`Scuba`,`AIM: 12 hours (no decompression stops) or 24 hours (stops) before flying up to 8,000 ft; 24 hours before going above 8,000.`]],
        exam: `Hyperventilation looks like hypoxia, and the fix is to slow down your breathing. Ear and sinus blocks are worst on descent.` },
      { h: `Carbon monoxide`,
        body: [`Biscuit's cabin heat is outside air run through a metal shroud wrapped around the exhaust muffler. It's simple and it works, and if the muffler cracks, your heat comes with exhaust in it. The AIM says heaters like that, with exhaust escaping through cracks and seals, cause several nonfatal and fatal accidents every year.`,
          `CO is colorless, odorless, and tasteless. It grabs hemoglobin about 200 times more easily than oxygen does, which is hypemic hypoxia, and the body can take up to 48 hours to clear it. If you smell exhaust, assume CO is there. If you smell nothing, that tells you nothing. Symptoms: headache, blurred vision, dizziness, drowsiness, loss of muscle power. Biscuit has a disposable detector card on the panel by the compass, and it darkens when there's CO around. Look at it the way you look at the oil pressure.`,
          `What you do: shut off the heater, open the fresh air vents and the window, use oxygen if you have it, and get on the ground. The AIM adds that if the symptoms are severe or don't go away after landing, get medical treatment. Then tell Marisol, because whatever made the CO is still in the airplane waiting for Russ. And smokers start the day behind. The handbook says smoking at sea level can put enough CO in your blood to feel like flying at 8,000 feet.`],
        terms: [[`Carbon monoxide`,`Colorless, odorless, tasteless. Binds hemoglobin about 200 times more readily than oxygen. Up to 48 hours to clear.`],[`CO response`,`Heater off, fresh air vents and windows open, oxygen if available, land. Seek treatment if symptoms continue.`],[`CO detector`,`Disposable card that changes color in the presence of CO.`]],
        exam: `Suspect CO with a headache, drowsiness, or dizziness while the heater's on. The first actions are heater off and fresh air.` },
      { h: `Your inner ear lies`,
        body: [`Three systems tell you which way is up. Your eyes. Your vestibular system, the fluid-filled semicircular canals and otolith organs in your inner ears. And your somatosensory system, the nerves in your skin, muscles, and joints, which is where seat of the pants flying lives. On a clear day your eyes win every argument. Lose the horizon, in a cloud, on a moonless night over the plains, and the other two get loud. They lie, and they sound sure.`,
          `The canals have a blind spot. Anything slower than about 2 degrees per second of rotation, they miss. And hold a turn long enough and the fluid catches up with the canal walls, so the turn stops registering at all. Most of the list comes out of that one fact. The leans: you've rolled slowly into a bank you never felt, you level the wings, and now you feel banked the other way, so you lean, or roll right back into the bank. Coriolis: long steady turn, you drop your head to grab the pencil off the floor, the fluid gets going on a new axis, and suddenly you're tumbling sideways through a sky that hasn't moved. The AIM says it's the most overwhelming of all illusions in flight. I believe them. Graveyard spiral: you're in a coordinated turn you can't feel, you see the altimeter unwinding, it feels like a wings-level descent, so you pull back. Pull in a bank and what you mostly get is more turn. Down it winds. Graveyard spin: same trick, played on you right after a spin recovery.`,
          `Then the otoliths, which sense straight-line acceleration and honestly can't tell it from tilt. Somatogravic illusion: shove the throttle in on takeoff and it feels like the nose is coming up, so the confused pilot pushes over toward the dirt. Yank the power back and it's reversed; they pull toward a stall. Inversion illusion: go abruptly from climb to level and it can feel like you're tumbling over backward, so they shove the nose down. Elevator illusion: an updraft feels like a climb, so they push. A downdraft feels like a dive, so they pull.`,
          `Your eyes have two of their own. A false horizon is a sloping cloud deck, or ground lights and stars smeared together at night, and you level the wings with the wrong thing. Autokinesis: stare at one light in the dark long enough and it starts to wander. Keep your eyes moving.`,
          `The cure is boring. Trust the instruments and ignore the feeling. The Airplane Flying Handbook's advice for a pilot without an instrument rating who ends up in cloud: the only concern at first is keeping the wings level, fly the attitude indicator with your fingertips, make small smooth changes, and use no more than 10 degrees of bank. And trying to fly half on the panel and half out the window, it says, is how people lose control. If you find yourself nose low with the airspeed climbing, the Instrument Flying Handbook gives the order: reduce power, level the wings with coordinated aileron and rudder, then raise the nose with smooth back pressure. Wings before pitch, same as a steep turn, for the same reason. It warns that the instinctive reaction to a nose-down attitude is to pull. That's the instinct you're training out of yourself.`,
          `Prevention beats all of it. Stay out of weather you can't see through until you're trained for it. And the handbook's list of things that make you more susceptible to disorientation reads like IMSAFE: illness, medication, alcohol, fatigue, sleep loss, mild hypoxia. The demonstration we'll do one Wednesday soon is one the handbook describes, flown with me in the airplane, gently, as high as the shelf lets us. You'll hate it. That's the point.`],
        terms: [[`The leans`,`After a slow, unnoticed bank, leveling the wings feels like banking the other way.`],[`Coriolis illusion`,`A head movement in a prolonged constant-rate turn creates a false sense of rotation on another axis. The most overwhelming illusion.`],[`Graveyard spiral`,`In an unfelt turn, a pilot sees altitude loss, feels wings level, pulls back, and tightens the spiral.`],[`Somatogravic illusion`,`Rapid acceleration feels like nose up; rapid deceleration feels like nose down.`],[`Nose-low recovery`,`Reduce power, level the wings, then raise the nose smoothly.`]],
        exam: `Match the illusion to the motion that causes it. For any disorientation question, the answer is to rely on the flight instruments.` },
      { h: `Runways that lie`,
        body: [`Some illusions don't spin you. They lie about the runway. A narrower than usual runway makes you feel higher than you are, so you fly a low approach and land short. A wider one makes you feel low, so you level out high and land hard or long. An upsloping runway or upsloping terrain makes you feel high, so you go low. Downslope does the opposite. Featureless terrain, water or snow or a dark field, makes you feel high, so you go low. Pilots call that a black hole approach.`,
          `Rain on the windshield makes you feel higher than you are. Haze makes the runway look farther away, so you end up low. And here's ours: extremely clear air at a high airport can make the runway look closer than it is, so you end up high and overshoot. Fog can feel like pitching up, so pilots steepen the approach, sometimes abruptly. Bright runway lights with nothing around them look closer, so you fly high. Lights along a straight road can look like a runway.`,
          `There's a pattern. Anything that makes you feel high makes you fly low, and low is the one that hits things. The fixes are dull and they work. Expect illusions at unfamiliar airports, especially at night. Read the Chart Supplement for runway slope and lighting. Look at your altimeter on every approach, day or night. Use the PAPI when there's one. Keep your landings sharp.`],
        terms: [[`Narrow runway`,`Feels high, so the pilot flies too low. Wide runway: feels low, pilot flies high.`],[`Upsloping runway or terrain`,`Feels high, so the pilot flies too low.`],[`Featureless terrain`,`Water, snow, darkness. Feels high, pilot flies low. The black hole approach.`],[`Haze`,`Runway seems farther away. Pilot tends to be low.`]],
        exam: `Classic question: a narrower than usual runway creates the illusion of being higher than you are, leading to a lower than normal approach.` },
      { h: `Five attitudes and the words that fix them`,
        body: [`The handbook names five attitudes that wreck decisions and gives each one an antidote. Memorize the antidotes word for word. That isn't me being fussy. The method is that when the thought shows up, you label it hazardous, then say the antidote, and that only works if the words come without effort.`,
          `Anti-authority: "Don't tell me." Rules, regulations, and procedures feel silly, and being told what to do makes you want to do the other thing. Antidote: Follow the rules. They are usually right. The handbook also says it's always your prerogative to question authority if you think it's in error. Question it. Then follow it while you do.`,
          `Impulsivity: "Do it quickly." Do something, anything, now, the first thing that comes to mind. Antidote: Not so fast. Think first.`,
          `Invulnerability: "It won't happen to me." You know accidents happen. You just don't feel like one is coming for you, so you take chances. Antidote: It could happen to me.`,
          `Macho: "I can do it." Trying to prove you're better than somebody, taking risks to impress. The handbook goes out of its way to say women are equally susceptible. Antidote: Taking chances is foolish.`,
          `Resignation: "What's the use?" You don't think you can change what happens, so you leave the action to others, or you go along with something unreasonable to be a nice guy. Antidote: I'm not helpless. I can make a difference.`,
          `You're going to want to tell me you don't have any of these. Everybody does, under the right pressure. You find yours by watching which one shows up when you're tired. Mine's macho, if you were wondering, and I'm aware of how that sounds from a flight instructor who tells you her hours every day.`],
        terms: [[`Anti-authority`,`"Don't tell me." Antidote: "Follow the rules. They are usually right."`],[`Impulsivity`,`"Do it quickly." Antidote: "Not so fast. Think first."`],[`Invulnerability`,`"It won't happen to me." Antidote: "It could happen to me."`],[`Macho`,`"I can do it." Antidote: "Taking chances is foolish."`],[`Resignation`,`"What's the use?" Antidote: "I'm not helpless. I can make a difference."`]],
        exam: `The test gives you a thought or a situation and asks for the attitude, or gives the attitude and asks for the antidote. The wording matters.` },
      { h: `PAVE, 3P, and DECIDE`,
        body: [`These are the tools for doing it on purpose. PAVE is how you look for hazards before a flight: Pilot in command, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures. Pilot is IMSAFE plus experience and currency. Aircraft is whether this airplane can do this trip: equipment, performance, fuel with reserves, the runway at the other end. Environment is weather, terrain, airports, airspace, and night. External pressures are everything outside the flight that wants it to happen: somebody waiting at the other end, a passenger you don't want to let down, wanting to show off, get-there-itis, pride. The handbook calls managing external pressure the single most important key to risk management, because it's the one category that can make you ignore the other three. Its fixes are all about leaving yourself a way out. Leave time for an extra stop. Have a backup plan. Tell the people waiting that you might be late. Carry a toothbrush.`,
          `3P is how you keep doing it once you're moving. Perceive the circumstances. Process them, by evaluating their effect on the safety of the flight. Perform, by doing the best thing. Then evaluate and go around the loop again. The handbook pairs it with two more lists. Process with CARE: consequences, alternatives, reality, external factors. Perform with TEAM: transfer, eliminate, accept, or mitigate. Transfer means handing the risk decision to someone better placed to make it, like your instructor. And the best sentence in the chapter is in the processing step: if you find yourself saying that it will probably be okay, it's definitely time for a solid reality check.`,
          `DECIDE is the other loop the test asks about: Detect that something changed, Estimate the need to react, Choose a course of action, Identify solutions, Do the necessary actions, Evaluate the effects. Then the handbook keeps a list of operational pitfalls, the traps that catch pilots with experience. Peer pressure. Mindset. Get-there-itis. Scud running. Continuing VFR into instrument conditions. Getting behind the aircraft. Losing situational awareness. Flying without adequate fuel reserves. Flying outside the envelope. Skipping the checklist because you've done it a thousand times.`,
          `Two biases the ACS names. Expectation bias is when you perceive what you expected instead of what's there. The Risk Management Handbook says it happens a lot on the ground, when a pilot who always gets the same taxi route hears the usual clearance instead of the one ATC gave. Confirmation bias is its cousin: information shows up that doesn't fit your plan, and you set it aside instead of letting it make you say uh-oh. The handbook adds that fatigue makes both worse, because a tired pilot stops taking the extra step to check.`],
        terms: [[`PAVE`,`Pilot in command, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures. Managing external pressure is the single most important key to risk management.`],[`3P`,`Perceive, Process, Perform, then evaluate. CARE to process, TEAM to perform.`],[`DECIDE`,`Detect, Estimate, Choose, Identify, Do, Evaluate.`],[`Expectation bias`,`Perceiving what you expected instead of what is actually there or said.`],[`Confirmation bias`,`Setting aside information that doesn't fit the plan.`]],
        exam: `Know what each PAVE letter covers, the 3P steps, and DECIDE. Get-there-itis is a pitfall, and external pressure is the PAVE category that feeds it.` },
      { h: `Personal minimums`,
        body: [`Last thing. The regulations are the floor. They don't promise you safety, and on a bad day they'll let you do things you'd never choose on a good one. Personal minimums are limits you set for yourself, tighter than the rules, based on your own experience and proficiency. The PHAK calls it the difference between what's legal and what's smart.`,
          `The Risk Management Handbook gives six steps. Review the weather categories: VFR is ceiling greater than 3,000 feet AGL and visibility greater than 5 miles, MVFR is 1,000 to 3,000 or 3 to 5 miles, IFR and LIFR below that. Assess your experience and comfort level from your logbook, honestly. Consider the other conditions: wind, gusts, crosswind, turbulence, shortest runway, highest terrain, highest density altitude. Assemble it into a baseline. Adjust the baseline for specific conditions. Stick to the plan.`,
          `The adjustment table is where eyes glaze, so look at it. If you're facing a pilot factor like illness, medication, stress, or fatigue, or an unfamiliar airplane, or an unfamiliar airport, or a must-get-there deadline, the handbook's starting points are to add at least 500 feet to your ceiling, at least half a mile to your visibility, at least 500 feet to the runway you need, and to take at least 5 knots off the winds you'll accept. If more than one applies, it says you can adjust for each one.`,
          `Then three cautions, and the first one is the reason the card exists. Don't adjust your personal minimums to complete a specific flight. The time to change them is when you have time and can be honest, not in the parking lot with somebody waiting. Change one variable at a time. Talk to an instructor before any big change. And write them down. The handbook says a written set makes it easier to explain a tough decision to passengers who are counting on you. It makes it easier to explain to yourself, too.`,
          `Your homework is still in your bag. I can see the corner of it from here.`],
        terms: [[`Personal minimums`,`Self-set limits more restrictive than the regulations, based on your experience and proficiency.`],[`RMH six steps`,`Review weather categories, assess experience, consider other conditions, assemble a baseline, adjust for conditions, stick to the plan.`],[`Adjustments`,`Per factor: at least +500 ft ceiling, +1/2 mile visibility, +500 ft runway, minus 5 knots of wind.`],[`First caution`,`Don't adjust personal minimums to complete a specific flight.`]],
        exam: `Personal minimums are set ahead of time and tightened, never loosened under pressure. VFR is a ceiling above 3,000 ft and visibility above 5 miles.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f8s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Wet cement`, q:`Jordan's congested, her second solo is at eight, and the cabinet has three medicines. How should she handle it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, October 23, 5:40 in the morning. Jordan woke with her head full of wet cement and her right ear crackling every time she swallowed. Her throat felt like she'd eaten a sandbox. It was the cold that had been going around her station for two weeks, the one the captain's kid brought home from kindergarten, and she'd been sure for three shifts running that she'd dodged it.`,`Solo at 8. Her second one ever. Three laps in the pattern, the ones Abby wanted until she was bored. The sky out the window was clean and pink over the plains, not a breath of wind.`,`The bathroom cabinet had the usual: a box of diphenhydramine, a nasal spray, and a blister pack of pseudoephedrine from last winter with two tablets left. She stood holding the cabinet door open and did what she did on a scene, which was say out loud what she had. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Congestion. Right ear won't clear. No fever."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take the diphenhydramine to dry her up, and fly at eight.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`She knows this drug. She's pushed it into a hundred allergic reactions and watched every one of them get sleepy. She takes it anyway, and at 7:15 she's on the highway with her eyelids heavy, and she texts Abby at a red light, which she never does. Abby: <b>what did you take.</b> She tells her. <b>Go home. That's in you well past tonight. Ask an AME before you fly on it again. I'll tell the schedule.</b>`,
          why:`The PHAK names diphenhydramine for drowsiness and a long half-life, and the AIM's safest rule is not to fly while taking any medication unless the FAA approves it. The cold itself is still there under the drug, with the ear block risk that comes with it.`},
        {t:`Use the nasal spray to open her ears, and fly at eight.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The spray works for about an hour. She gets as far as the runup on Alpha before her right ear is a drum again, and she sits there short of the hold lines and remembers that the coming down is the part that hurts. She tells ground she'd like to taxi back to parking, and calls Abby from the tie down. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Good catch. Late. Next time catch it at the cabinet."</span>`,
          why:`The AIM says decongestant sprays usually don't give adequate protection against an ear block, which happens on descent. A thousand feet down with a blocked Eustachian tube can still hurt and damage the eardrum, and the AIM's safest rule is not to fly with any illness.`},
        {t:`Text Abby that she's sick and cancel the eight o'clock.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`She types it before she's out of the bathroom. <b>Sick. Head cold, right ear won't clear. Canceling 8am.</b> Abby, in under a minute: <b>ok. feel better.</b> Then: <b>what were you going to take.</b> Jordan looks at the cabinet. <b>Nothing now.</b> <b>good answer. that's the AIM's safest rule in one text.</b> She makes tea, goes back to bed, and sleeps until noon. Russ takes the slot and sends a picture of pancakes from the airport diner.`,
          why:`The AIM's safest rule is not to fly with any illness, and congestion can cause an ear or sinus block on descent. Masking symptoms with medication adds side effects without removing the illness. Canceling costs a morning.`},
        {t:`Take the non-drowsy pseudoephedrine before the eight o'clock.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`By 7 she's jittery, heart going a little fast, and she catches herself counting it at a red light out of habit, 108. At the wing Abby asks the question, and Jordan tells the truth, because she's bad at the other thing. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Non-drowsy isn't the same as fine. And the cold's still in there under it. Home."</span>`,
          why:`The AIM says oral decongestants have side effects that can significantly impair pilot performance, and its safest rule is not to fly with any illness. The cold is still there under the pill and can still block an ear on descent. A non-drowsy label isn't a clearance to fly.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s2`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`IX`, title:`The card by the compass`, q:`With the heater on, Jordan has a headache and the CO card has darkened. What should she do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, October 29, the first real cold of the year: minus three on the ramp, frost feathered on Biscuit's wings that Jordan wiped off with a towel and then checked by hand, every inch, because Abby had made her promise. Runup on Alpha near the end of 35R, mags, carb heat, both latches. She told ground she was ready for departure and ground told her to monitor tower. On the climbout she pulled the cabin heat knob all the way out, and warm air came up around her ankles smelling like hot metal and dust, which Marisol had said was normal for the first cold week.`,`Second lap. Downwind, 6,900. Somewhere around the turn to base she noticed a headache had moved in behind her eyes, the dull kind, and that she'd yawned twice. She'd slept eight hours. On final she looked at the little CO detector card stuck to the panel next to the compass. It was supposed to be pale. The dot in the middle had gone the gray of an old nickel.`,`Under her feet the heater kept blowing.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Heat off and fresh air in, then a full stop on this lap.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Knob in. Both vents open, the little storm window on her door cracked, and minus three comes in like a slap, which is the point. She keeps flying the final. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Tower, Niner One Echo, request full stop, possible exhaust in the cabin."</span> <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Niner One Echo, runway three five right, cleared to land. Do you need assistance?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Negative, Niner One Echo."</span> She lands, taxis in with the window open, shuts down, and sits on the step breathing. Marisol has the cowling off in ten minutes and the heat shroud off in twenty. A crack in the muffler about an inch long, black around the edges. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Finally,"</span> Marisol says. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Something actually broken."</span> Marisol leaves the cowling on the floor and drives her to the station, where her partner puts the CO probe from the monitor on her finger. Seven percent. Her partner whistles. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"That's a smoker number."</span> By noon the headache's gone, and Biscuit's grounded until Marisol finds a muffler.`,
          why:`AIM 8-1-4 and the PHAK: with symptoms or a darkened detector while the heater's on, suspect CO, shut off the heater, open fresh air vents and windows, use oxygen if available, and land. Get checked if symptoms continue. Whatever leaked is a maintenance problem for the next pilot too.`},
        {t:`Keep the heat on and crack a vent until the headache eases.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The headache doesn't ease. On the third downwind she realizes she's skipped carb heat abeam the numbers for the first time since June and doesn't remember deciding to. She lands long and slow and shuts down, and Marisol smells it at the door from three feet away. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Out. Sit on the grass. Don't argue with me."</span>`,
          why:`A cracked vent doesn't stop CO pouring in through a heater that's still on. The source has to be shut off. CO causes drowsiness and slowed thinking, which is why the missed checklist item showed up.`},
        {t:`Turn the heat off, open the vents, and fly the planned last lap.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She flies the third lap with the heat off and the cabin going cold, and lands fine. Marisol, after the shroud is off and the crack is in plain sight: <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Off was right. Another lap with that in your blood was not. You don't do extra credit with CO."</span>`,
          why:`Turning off the heater is the right first step, and opening vents should come with it. But CO can take up to 48 hours to clear from the body, so symptoms don't stop when the heater does. Land as soon as practical.`},
        {t:`Ignore the card. Those cheap ones go gray with age.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`She flies the third lap anyway, and the headache gets worse, and on the ground Marisol looks at the card and then at her. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"That card's two weeks old. I stuck it there myself. Gray means gray."</span> She has the cowling off before Jordan's done unbuckling.`,
          why:`The PHAK says CO detectors change color to warn of CO, and CO can be dangerous with no smell at all. A headache and drowsiness with the heater on are the classic signs. Explaining away a warning is how this becomes an accident.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s3`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Ellie is Biscuit`, q:`A beer's on offer, and Jordan's next solo is early tomorrow. What's her call?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, October 31. Ellie went as Biscuit.`,`It was a moving box out of Claire's garage. Arm holes. Two cardboard wings, duct tape. N4791E down the side in black Sharpie, the 4 a little backward, plus a maroon stripe in marker. The propeller was a paper plate on a brass fastener, and it spun if you blew on it. On the front, where the panel would be, she'd written HI DAD in purple. She walked the whole cul de sac in Parker with a pillowcase and told every adult who opened a door that the tachometer read fifty high.`,`At the house afterward Claire got two bottles out of the fridge and held them up like evidence. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Have a beer with me. I've earned one and so have you."</span> On the fridge door, under a magnet from a pizza place, was a school picture of Ellie in a green sweater, third grade, with two curled corners of old yellow tape still stuck to it. The tape caught Jordan's eye. She couldn't have said why.`,`Her next solo was 7 in the morning. The clocks went back tonight. She'd already thought about that, twice.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Not tonight, Claire. I'm flying at seven." Just water.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Claire puts one back. The other she pops on the edge of the counter, Danny's trick, the one she always said she hated. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"You get a free hour tonight with the clocks."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I'm spending it on sleep."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"I see what 'I waited eight hours' looks like at two in the morning. Everybody treats it like a target."</span> From inside the box, Ellie: <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Is that a rule or a you rule?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Dry, deliberate, a little amused.">"A me rule."</span> Ellie considers this, propeller turning slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Those are the good ones."</span>`,
          why:`91.17 sets 8 hours and 0.04 as the legal lines, and the AIM recommends 12 to 24 hours between bottle and throttle. Zero drinks before an early flight is the simplest way to meet every part of the rule and the recommendation.`},
        {t:`Two beers, the second finished by nine, ten hours before she flies.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Two beers, and she counts them, and she's in bed by ten. In the morning she feels fine, and she is legal by a wide margin. Abby, when she mentions it later, just says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Legal. The AIM says twelve to twenty four. Ten's legal. It isn't smart."</span> With the clocks going back it was eleven real hours, which Jordan figured out in the car and found she didn't feel any better about.`,
          why:`More than 8 hours is legal under 91.17 if the alcohol concentration is under 0.04 and she isn't impaired. But the AIM recommends 12 to 24 hours depending on the amount, and the PHAK says to be more conservative than the regulations.`},
        {t:`Three beers, done by 11:20, over eight hours with the time change.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`At 5:30 she wakes with a dry mouth and a head like a shoebox. She does the math on the back of a receipt: legal by forty minutes, on the real clock. Then she thinks about what the PHAK says about hangovers and texts Abby. <b>Canceling 7am. Hungover-ish.</b> Abby: <b>ish is a yes. thanks for telling me.</b>`,
          why:`8 hours and 40 minutes meets the 8 hour part of 91.17, but a hangover is still under the influence of alcohol, which 91.17(a)(2) prohibits. The PHAK says about 3 hours per drink, and the AIM recommends 12 to 24 hours.`},
        {t:`One big glass of wine instead of the beers she's offered.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Claire pours it into a glass the size of a fishbowl. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"That's a nurse pour."</span> Jordan looks at it and does the math in ounces, and it comes out to two drinks and change. She drinks a third of it and pours the rest back.`,
          why:`The PHAK's table gives 4 ounces of table wine about the same alcohol as a 12 ounce light beer, about half an ounce. So a big pour of wine is more than one drink.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s4`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Thirteen five`, q:`Russ plans a long leg at 13,500 feet with no oxygen. How should Jordan answer?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday, November 1, the first morning on Mountain Standard Time, and the club chat woke up before Jordan did.`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="8:48 AM">booked biscuit friday the 13th, KAPA to steamboat, back sunday. muffler's done right marisol</prop>`,`<prop type="chat" from="Marisol Tran" time="8:52 AM">New muffler, new gasket, shroud's clean. Ran it yesterday. The CO card is new too. Look at it.</prop>`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="9:03 AM">back over the divide sunday at 13,500, about 45 min up there. no O2, i live at 5,800 feet, i'm basically a sherpa. jordan want to ride along? you can't log it but there's a burrito place</prop>`,`${(s.picks||{})[2]===2?`Jordan read it in bed with the blinds still down.`:`Jordan read it at the tie down with the engine still ticking.`} Walt hadn't said anything yet, which meant he was typing.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him to bring oxygen and use it after 30 minutes above 12,500.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She types it with the section number, because she's her. <b>91.211(a)(1). Over 12,500 up to 14,000, crew has to use O2 for the part over 30 min. 45 min up there is 15 on oxygen. Also hypoxia's first symptom is feeling great. Sherpas carry oxygen.</b> Walt: <b>Thank you, Jordan.</b> Marisol: <b>Portable oxygen is in the cabinet, full, cannulas and the finger oximeter in the drawer. I'll show you Thursday, Russ.</b> A long pause. Russ: <b>cool. cool cool cool.</b> Then: <b>fine. i'll wear the nose hose. still a sherpa. jordan?</b> She's working that Friday, the shift she traded for. She tells him to bring her a burrito.`,
          why:`91.211(a)(1) requires the required minimum flight crew to use supplemental oxygen for any time beyond 30 minutes at cabin altitudes above 12,500 up to and including 14,000. The PHAK says everyone is susceptible regardless of acclimatization, and euphoria can be the first symptom.`},
        {t:`Tell him to plan the leg at 12,500 or lower the whole way.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Walt, a minute later: <b>Legal. Look at the ridgelines on that route before you pick it.</b> Russ looks, and sends back a screenshot of the sectional with a lot of numbers on it that start with 12 and 13. <b>ok i see the problem.</b> Marisol: <b>Oxygen's in the cabinet.</b>`,
          why:`At or below 12,500 no supplemental oxygen is required by 91.211, but the Divide has terrain that leaves very little clearance at that altitude, and the AIM recommends oxygen above 10,000 feet by day. Legal and wise aren't the same calculation.`},
        {t:`Say yes to the burrito and trust that he'll feel it if anything's off.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Walt answers before she can take it back. <b>He won't feel it. That's the problem with it.</b> Then, to Russ: <b>91.211. Look it up or I'll read it to you at the meeting.</b> Russ sends a thumbs up that somehow manages to sound hurt.`,
          why:`The PHAK says all pilots are susceptible to hypoxia regardless of endurance or acclimatization, and its early effects can feel like well-being. 45 minutes between 12,500 and 14,000 also requires the crew to use oxygen under 91.211(a)(1).`},
        {t:`Tell him 13,500 is fine without oxygen, under the 14,000 line.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Walt: <b>Read (a)(1) again.</b> She does, with her phone an inch from her face. Above 12,500 up to and including 14,000. More than 30 minutes. She types <b>never mind, I'm wrong</b>, and Walt sends back <b>That's the most useful sentence in aviation.</b>`,
          why:`Above 14,000 the crew uses oxygen the entire time, but the band above 12,500 up to and including 14,000 also requires crew oxygen for the portion over 30 minutes. A 45 minute leg at 13,500 needs 15 minutes of it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s5`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Sleep, meds, sick, anything`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, November 4. She'd slept from eleven to three, which was four hours, and then she'd been on the kitchen floor with her back against the dishwasher, listening to the voicemail.`,`It was forty one seconds long, from the Thursday before. She'd never deleted it and she'd never played it, both on purpose, until three this morning. <i>Hey. It's me. Ellie's got a game Saturday at nine, the fields by the rec center. Come if you're off. She's playing defense now, she's terrifying. Okay. Love you. Bye.</i> She hadn't been off. She'd been on a twenty four, and at 9:40 that Saturday her phone had buzzed in the rig with Claire's name on it. She played it twice. Then she got up and made coffee because it was 3:20 and there wasn't any point.`,`Tomorrow was his birthday.`,`6:50 AM at the tie down. Minus one, dead calm, frost on the wing that she'd toweled off and checked by hand already. Abby came around the tail with her coffee and the hood bag over one shoulder and asked the thing she always asked, same words every time. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Sleep, meds, sick, anything?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Four hours, and tomorrow's his birthday. I want to fly. You should know that first."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:2,health:4},
          after:`Abby sets her coffee down on the wing, carefully. She doesn't say she's sorry. Thank God. She just presses her lips together, the face she makes adding up hours on the back of a fuel receipt. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Gentle, steady, businesslike.">"Thank you. Okay, here's the deal. Dual, which it was anyway. Some hood work, nothing fancy, no pattern work alone. The minute it isn't there, you say so out loud and we go home. That still counts as a good lesson. Deal?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quiet, sober, steady.">"Deal."</span> Abby picks the coffee back up. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Gentle, steady, businesslike.">"Out loud. I'll hold you to that part."</span>`,
          why:`Fatigue and Emotion are both on IMSAFE, and the AIM says a pilot shouldn't fly after an emotionally upsetting event until recovered. Telling the instructor transfers part of the risk decision to someone who can see it from outside, the T in TEAM, and lets her set a clear way out.`},
        {t:`"Fine. Slept fine." Flying will take her mind off it, and she isn't going to explain the rest.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Good,"</span> says Abby, already reaching for the tow bar. First lie Jordan's ever told her. It was easy, and the easy part is what sticks with her all through the preflight. She checks the oil twice. She can't remember the first time.`,
          why:`Hiding fatigue and grief from the instructor removes the one person who could help weigh them. Using a flight to escape an emotional event is the situation the AIM warns about: emotions reduce alertness and can push risk taking.`},
        {t:`"A little short on sleep, nothing else." It's true, and the birthday isn't Abby's business.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"How short?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Some."</span> Abby looks at her a second longer than she usually would, coffee halfway up, then nods. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Easy lesson, then. Tell me if it isn't."</span> Jordan says she will. Whether she means it, she's not totally sure.`,
          why:`Mentioning fatigue is better than nothing, but leaving out a known emotional stressor gives the instructor half the picture. IMSAFE's E is there because emotion affects performance even when you feel functional.`},
        {t:`"Ask me after. I'll know better once I'm in the airplane and the engine's running."`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's not where the checklist goes."</span> She waits, and waits, and finally Jordan says she's tired. Abby says <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Okay. Dual. Easy,"</span> and they go. On the taxi out to Alpha, Abby watches her hands more than she watches the taxiway, and Jordan notices, and neither of them brings it up.`,
          why:`IMSAFE is a preflight check, done on the ground where stopping is free. The PHAK points out that a tired pilot asked if they're too tired may say no, so the assessment gets worse, not better, once you're busy flying.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s6`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`VIII`, title:`Eyes closed`, q:`What's the correct recovery from a nose low, banked unusual attitude?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`7:50 AM, 7,500 feet over the stubble fields east of Bennett, about two thousand above the ground, the shelf at 8,000 overhead, and the sun low and white in the windshield. Abby had her under the hood, the foggles that cut off everything but the panel, for twenty minutes of straight and level and shallow turns, and Jordan had been fine. Better than fine. The attitude indicator was the only thing in the world, and it was very calm.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Okay. Demo. I have the airplane."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"You have the airplane."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Eyes closed. Chin on your chest. Tell me what you feel."</span>`,`She closed her eyes. Dark. Engine noise in her teeth. Level, it felt like, for a long while, then maybe a climb, then nothing she could name. For no reason her head went to a soccer field she'd never actually stood on, and then to the dishwasher at three in the morning. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Head up. Eyes open. Recover. Your airplane."</span>`,`The attitude indicator showed the little airplane banked thirty degrees right, nose well below the horizon. Airspeed 115 and climbing. Altimeter 7,320 and unwinding. Every nerve in her body said wings level and a gentle descent.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pull back firmly to stop the descent, then check the wings.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`She pulls. Her seat gets heavy, the turn winds tighter, and the airspeed just sits at 120. 7,200. 7,150. Abby has the throttle and the yoke at once. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Graveyard spiral. Pull in a bank and mostly you turn. Wings first."</span> Abby levels it herself. Jordan's hands go to her lap and shake a little there.`,
          why:`In an unfelt bank, pulling back tightens the turn and steepens the descent, which is the graveyard spiral. The Instrument Flying Handbook warns that the instinct in a nose-low attitude is to pull. Level the wings first.`},
        {t:`Power back, wings level on the attitude indicator, then nose up.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Power back. Left aileron, some left rudder, eyes glued to the little airplane, and her whole body yells that she's rolling into a bank. She does it anyway. Wings level on the gauge. Back pressure, smooth. The airspeed bleeds off; the altimeter slows, stops at 7,240, turns around. Power in. 7,500. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Power, wings, pitch. In that order. That's all it is."</span> Inside her gloves her hands are wet. She never felt the bank. Not a degree of it.`,
          why:`The Instrument Flying Handbook's nose-low recovery: reduce power, correct the bank with coordinated aileron and rudder by reference to the instruments, then raise the nose with smooth back pressure. The AFH: believe the instruments regardless of what your senses say.`},
        {t:`Level the wings, then raise the nose, and leave the throttle alone.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Wings come level on the gauge. The nose is slower, and by then the airspeed reads 125 with the engine howling, so Abby eases the throttle back for her. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Wings, right order. Nose low and fast, though, power comes off first. Otherwise you're diving with good posture."</span>`,
          why:`Leveling the wings before raising the nose is correct. But in a nose-low attitude with airspeed climbing, the first step is reducing power to prevent excessive airspeed and altitude loss.`},
        {t:`Lift the foggles and fix the attitude by the real horizon outside.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Abby's hand is on the foggles before Jordan's is. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"In a cloud there's no outside. Fly the panel."</span> Later, on the ground, while Jordan's still pulling the tow bar: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Half on the panel, half out the window. That's how people lose it. The book says it in almost those words, I'm not being dramatic."</span>`,
          why:`The demonstration teaches recovery by instruments, because in real instrument conditions there's nothing to look at. The AFH says controlling partly by instruments while searching outside for confirmation leads to loss of control.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s7`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Not here`, q:`Jordan has missed traffic and busted Abby's altitude, and now she's crying a little. What's her best move?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`It went on like that.`,`She flew the heading back toward the field and it wandered. Abby called traffic, a Cherokee at two o'clock low, and Jordan didn't hear it until the second time, and then couldn't find it, and then found it much closer than she liked. In a shallow climbing turn to give it room she let the nose come up, and when she looked, the altimeter said 7,780. Eighty feet over Abby's cap. Two hundred and twenty under the shelf. Abby tapped the glass with one fingernail and said nothing, which was worse.`,`Jordan fixed it. Seven seven hundred, seven six, level at 7,500. Her hands were fine. Her hands were always fine, that had never been the problem. Then the panel went blurry at the edges. She blinked and it came back, and then it went again, and it took her a good second to get that she was crying. A little. None of the rest of it, no noise, just the water, the way you sweat on a hot call.`,`Fifteen miles from the field. Forty minutes left on the lesson plan: hood work home, then three landings.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"I'm not here today. Let's go home." She says it out loud, and they fly straight back.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:2,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Lower, quiet, almost kind.">"Okay. Good."</span> Abby doesn't take the controls. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Lower, quiet, almost kind.">"Your airplane, your radios. Straight back, no hood. You're doing the exact thing, by the way. This is the thing."</span> Jordan wipes her face with the back of her glove and flies a heading of 270, and it holds. In the logbook that afternoon, in green, Abby writes <b>LESSON TERMINATED BY STUDENT. GOOD CALL.</b>`,
          why:`The AIM says a pilot shouldn't fly after an emotionally upsetting event until recovered, and the errors were stacking up. Saying it out loud uses the instructor as a resource and ends the risk at its source. 3P: she perceived it, processed it, and performed.`},
        {t:`Push through it like a bad call at work and finish the lesson plan as briefed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I'm fine. Hood home."</span> The hood home is worse. She chases the heading needle ten degrees each way and loses two hundred feet in a turn she can't feel, and three miles later Abby reaches over and lifts the hood off her herself. Then she says, flatly, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Last leg's yours, no hood. We're done after this one."</span>`,
          why:`"Stopping means I failed" is the macho attitude, I can do it, and its antidote is "Taking chances is foolish." The emotional event and the errors were real hazards, and the fix was to stop, not to try harder.`},
        {t:`Ask Abby to fly ten minutes while she gets herself together, then do the landings.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Abby flies. Jordan breathes, and counts the breaths, and gets to about forty before Abby looks over at her face. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ten minutes won't get you recovered. That's okay."</span> Then, flatly: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Last leg's yours, no hood. We're done after this one."</span>`,
          why:`Handing off the controls is a good use of resources. But the AIM's standard is to not fly until satisfactorily recovered from an emotional event, and ten minutes rarely gets you there. End the lesson.`},
        {t:`Say nothing. Skip the hood, fly extra carefully, and do the landings as planned.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Abby lets it go about a mile. Then she reaches over and touches Jordan's sleeve. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You're crying and you're eighty feet over my cap. Saying nothing isn't flying carefully. We're going home."</span> In the logbook that afternoon, in green: <b>LESSON TERMINATED BY INSTRUCTOR.</b>`,
          why:`Keeping quiet hides the hazard from the one other person who can help manage it. The errors were already showing up, and "fly extra carefully" isn't a plan for impairment you can't fully see.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s8`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Three five right`, q:`Jordan's runway readback went uncorrected, but something nags at her. What should she do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Twelve miles east, 7,500, she listened to the ATIS on the way in. Information Echo, one five one five Zulu. Wind three five zero at eight, visibility one zero, sky clear, temperature zero, dew point minus seven, altimeter three zero one eight. Landing and departing runway three five left. Runway one seven left, three five right, closed.`,`She'd written the NOTAM on her kneeboard at the kitchen table that morning at 5:40, after the coffee, before the tie down: <b>35R CLOSED 1500 ZULU TO 2300 ZULU.</b> And next to it, in her own hand, <b>= 9 AM.</b> She'd subtracted six. It was November.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Tower, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, twelve east, seven thousand five hundred, landing with Echo."</span> <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Niner One Echo, Centennial Tower, make right base runway three five left, report two mile right base."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Right base, three five right, report two mile base, Niner One Echo."</span> She heard herself say it. Nobody corrected her. The frequency was busy, a Citation and two students and somebody's long question about a taxi route. Abby, in the right seat, was looking out her window with great interest at nothing. Ahead, the long runway was coming up in the windshield the way it always did, the one she'd landed on two hundred times, with the shorter one beyond it, and something in the back of her head said <i>left</i>, and then <i>closed</i>, and then went quiet.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask tower to verify the runway before she turns final, and fly whatever they confirm.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Tower, Niner One Echo, verify runway three five left?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Niner One Echo, affirmative, runway three five left. Three five right is closed. Report two mile right base."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Three five left, Niner One Echo."</span> She looks down at her kneeboard. <b>= 9 AM.</b> Fifteen hundred Zulu was eight o'clock. The runway she'd been aiming at had been closed for twenty minutes. Danny's line from the TAF entry came up word for word: <i>In the winter it's seven, which I will also forget.</i> She had. Abby, still looking out her window: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Expectation bias, plus Zulu, plus four hours of sleep. You caught it. Fly the left one."</span>`,
          why:`A readback that ATC doesn't correct isn't a confirmation, especially on a busy frequency. When something nags, ask ATC to verify. The RMH describes expectation bias as hearing the usual clearance instead of the one given, and fatigue makes it worse.`},
        {t:`Fly to 35R. She read it back, tower didn't correct her, and that's how a clearance works.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`She rolls out on a final for 35R and sees, too late to feel good about it, a line of orange cones across the far end and a sweeper truck on the centerline. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Sharp, firm, urgent.">"Niner One Echo, go around, runway three five right is closed. I said three five left."</span> Abby's hand is on the throttle with hers. Full power, carb heat off, climb. Jordan looks at her kneeboard on the upwind. <b>= 9 AM.</b> It was November. Seven, not six.`,
          why:`Silence after a wrong readback isn't approval. Busy controllers miss readback errors. The ATIS and the NOTAM both said 35R was closed. Expectation bias put her on the runway she always lands on instead of the one assigned.`},
        {t:`Ask Abby which runway she heard tower say, and fly the one Abby confirms.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ask the person who gave it."</span> So she does, and tower says three five left, three five right is closed. On the ground Abby says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Asking me is fine today. On a solo there's nobody in this seat. Make the radio your habit."</span>`,
          why:`The instructor is a resource, but the clearance comes from ATC, and only ATC can confirm it. On a solo there's no one else to ask. When in doubt, ask the controller to verify.`},
        {t:`Line up on 35L without a word. If 35R is closed she must have heard left, so it's fine.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She turns for the left runway. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Niner One Echo, verify you're for three five left."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Affirmative, Niner One Echo."</span> Abby, after: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Right runway. Wrong way to get there. Tower thought you were going to the closed one, because that's what you told them."</span>`,
          why:`Her readback told ATC she was going to 35R. Quietly flying something different leaves the controller with a wrong picture. Resolve the doubt on the radio before acting on a guess.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s9`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Window`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday night she drove out to Parker with a quart of her mother's green chile on the passenger seat, because Lourdes had made too much for tomorrow and had decided Claire wasn't eating. Claire had just got in from the ER and was still in ceil blue, eating cereal over the counter.`,`Ellie came down in airplane pajamas. New ones, Jordan was almost sure. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie" data-tone="Quiet, earnest, curious.">"Mom said you had a bad flying day because you're sad about Daddy's birthday."</span> She sat on the bottom step to think about it. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie" data-tone="Quiet, earnest, curious.">"Is sad a kind of tired?"</span> Jordan looked at Claire. Claire looked at the cereal. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Soft, quiet, honest.">"Yeah, El. It is."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ellie" data-tone="Quiet, earnest, curious.">"Okay. That's what I thought."</span> And she went back up, and Jordan had to sit down on the step she'd left.`,`Later, on the back step, with tea neither of them drank, Claire said it. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Dry, rueful, quietly clear.">"Four hundred and ten dollars a month, for eleven years. I did it on a calculator in March at three in the morning, which is when I do math now. Fifty four thousand one hundred and twenty dollars, before gas, before his part of the annuals. And Saturdays. Every Saturday morning I ever wanted, that airplane got first."</span> She turned the mug in her hands. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Dry, rueful, quietly clear.">"The one Saturday he skipped it was her game. He'd missed three in a row and he felt bad. So."</span> She didn't finish, and Jordan didn't make her. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Dry, rueful, quietly clear.">"I've been mad at a Cessna for seven months. It's exhausting. It didn't do anything."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Claire">"And he got this thing. You only ever got the phone calls after. He called it a window. 'There's a window.' When Danny said window I started packing the car. The night before a trip he'd check the weather every ten minutes, and when it got worse he didn't cancel, he moved the time. Seven became six. Six became wheels up by five thirty. Once I found him in the garage at four in the morning loading the cooler in the dark so nobody would wake up and argue. He had a voice for it. Bright. Like a kid telling you about the lamp before you find it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Claire">"My sister's wedding, in Durango. The window over Wolf Creek was closing, so he wanted to go a day early, then he wanted to go that night. I said I'd drive. He flew alone, and he beat me there by six hours and met me in the parking lot with a coffee and that face."</span> The short laugh. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"He was always fine. That's the thing about it. Every time he was fine, the next one got easier. You chart enough probably fine and you stop seeing it."</span> She put the mug down on the step between them. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Direct, quiet, clear.">"So I'm going to ask you, because nobody ever asked him. Do you have it? The fever?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"This morning I did. I said I was fine. I'm writing my rules down now, before I need them."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Claire nods slowly, looking at the dark yard. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, even, clear.">"He wrote it all down after. Every time. Funny, so nobody would worry."</span> She picks the mug back up. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, even, clear.">"Write yours before."</span> They sit there until the motion light over the garage clicks off. Neither of them gets up to wave at it. Somebody's dog two yards over barks about it for a while and then quits.`,
          why:`Personal minimums written ahead of time are the RMH's tool for exactly this, set when time and objectivity allow, never adjusted to make a flight happen. Admitting the hazard is the first step in the ADM process.`},
        {t:`"No. I'm not him. I'm the careful one in this family, and today was one bad day."`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Claire smiles without any of the rest of her face. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"That's what he'd say. Word for word, almost."</span> She picks up her mug and goes in. Jordan sits on the step by herself for a while longer, cold coming up through her jeans, which she figures was sort of the point.`,
          why:`"It won't happen to me" is invulnerability, and the antidote is "It could happen to me." The morning had already shown the hazard. Being careful by reputation isn't a control.`},
        {t:`"Maybe. I'll stop flying for a while, until his birthday and March are both behind me."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Claire">"That's not what I asked."</span> She looks at Jordan sideways. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"March comes every year, Jordan. So does tomorrow. You're going to need something better than hiding from a calendar."</span>`,
          why:`Not flying on hard days is reasonable, but stopping indefinitely avoids the skill instead of building it. The better tool is a written personal minimum that names the hazard, such as emotional dates as an IMSAFE item, before you need it.`},
        {t:`"Everybody has a little. If you're scared of every cloud you'll never fly anywhere."`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Claire goes very still. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"That's the voice,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"That's it exactly."</span> She goes inside. Through the kitchen window Jordan can see her at the sink, water off, both hands on the counter, not doing a thing.`,
          why:`Normalizing risk because it usually works out is what Claire just described, and the PHAK's warning sign is the word probably. Managing external pressure and your own get-there-itis is the most important key to risk management.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s10`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Forty two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, November 5. Lunch at her mother's was pozole in the good bowls, and Lourdes set out five of them before she stopped, and stood there, and put one back in the cabinet without a word. Nobody said happy birthday. Her mother said the hominy was the wrong brand this year, twice, and that was how they said it.`,`At seven, the November safety meeting. Hangar Six, nine folding chairs, eight people. On the folding table by the coffee was a pink box of donuts, a dozen, and Walt stood next to it and didn't explain it. After a minute Marisol said, to nobody, <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Danny brought those every year. Closest meeting to his birthday. Said it was a tax."</span> Russ took one and held it without eating it.`,`Before he started, Walt looked at Jordan over his glasses. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"${(s.picks||{})[6]===0?`Abby wrote 'terminated by student' in your book yesterday. First time I've seen that line in this club.`:`Abby wrote 'terminated' in your book yesterday. Read it before next month.`}"</span> Then he turned to the whiteboard, under Danny's capitals and Marisol's, and wrote five words: ANTI-AUTHORITY. IMPULSIVITY. INVULNERABILITY. MACHO. RESIGNATION.`,`He read from an index card. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I read a report like this once. Doesn't matter where. There's a TFR for a wildfire twenty miles west of a pilot's home field. Pilot knows about it. Says, and it's in the report, 'TFRs are for news helicopters. Nobody tells me where I can fly my own airplane.' Flies straight through it to get a look."</span> He capped the marker. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Jordan. Which one, and the antidote. Word for word. The test wants the words."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Anti-authority. They resent being told where to fly. "Follow the rules. They are usually right."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`Walt writes it under ANTI-AUTHORITY in his small neat capitals. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Good. You can question a rule. You file a question. You don't fly through it."</span> He goes through the other four with cases of his own, and nobody eats a donut until he's done, and then everybody does. On the way out he stops beside her chair. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Which one's yours? You don't have to say it here."</span> She doesn't. In the car she writes it on the back of her hand with a pen from the glove box, the way Danny used to: MACHO. TAKING CHANCES IS FOOLISH.`,
          why:`"Nobody tells me" and treating a TFR as silly is anti-authority, "Don't tell me." The PHAK antidote is "Follow the rules. They are usually right." You can always question a rule you think is wrong, through the right channels.`},
        {t:`Macho, out to prove they're good enough to fly right past a fire. "Taking chances is foolish."`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"There's some of that in them."</span> He taps the card. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"But listen to what they said. Nobody tells me. That's the tell. When the reason is the rule itself, it's anti-authority. Follow the rules. They are usually right."</span>`,
          why:`Macho is about proving yourself and impressing others. This pilot's own words point at resenting the rule: "Nobody tells me where I can fly." That's anti-authority, with the antidote "Follow the rules. They are usually right."`},
        {t:`Invulnerability, sure that nothing will happen to them out there. "It could happen to me."`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"They probably think that too. Most of them stack."</span> He points at the card. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"But they told you which one was driving. Nobody tells me. Anti-authority. Follow the rules. They are usually right."</span>`,
          why:`Hazardous attitudes often overlap, and they likely felt invulnerable too. But the stated reason, "Nobody tells me where I can fly," is anti-authority, and the test asks for the dominant attitude.`},
        {t:`Resignation, as if it were all out of their hands. "I'm not helpless. I can make a difference."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Walt waits a second, in case she wants it back. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Resignation is the pilot who gives up and lets things happen to them. This pilot did the opposite. They decided, and they decided wrong. Anti-authority."</span>`,
          why:`Resignation is "What's the use?", leaving the action to others. This pilot acted deliberately against a rule they called pointless, which is anti-authority, with the antidote "Follow the rules. They are usually right."`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s11`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Every box`, q:`Jordan has 1.6 solo hours, all in the pattern, and a blank minimums card. How should she fill it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, November 6, two in the afternoon, the briefing room. Abby had texted the night before, after the meeting: <b>Bring the worksheet. Tomorrow. I'll sit there while you do it.</b>`,`The packet was where it had been for two weeks, in her flight bag, under the green notebook, with a coffee ring on the cover she didn't remember making. Abby slid a pencil across the table and a white index card, lined on one side. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Worksheet's the thinking. Card's what goes in the airplane. Fill the worksheet from your logbook. Then the card."</span>`,`Her logbook: 43.0 hours. Alone, 1.6, every tenth of it in the Centennial pattern. Windiest landing by herself, eight knots. With Abby, fourteen gusting twenty, and she'd rather not do that again soon. Shortest runway, 28, 4,800 feet. Highest density altitude, about 9,600, one August afternoon she'd worked out on a legal pad with Abby watching.`,`Abby didn't watch. She had her own logbook open and was adding again.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Build it from her logbook, inside Abby's limits, with sleep and shift lines.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She works for forty minutes. The experience table first, every box, even the ones that say zero. Then the baseline. Then the adjustments, and she writes the handbook's rule at the bottom of the worksheet so she'll see it: don't change these to make a flight happen. Then the card, slowly, in block capitals. Abby reads it twice. No cross outs. One tap of her pencil on one line. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"This one. Most people don't write that one."</span> Then, at the bottom: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Two factors, go home. That's tighter than the book. I'll take it."</span> She hands the card back. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"So where's it going?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Airplane."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Where in the airplane?"</span> Jordan doesn't answer that one yet.`,
          why:`The RMH builds personal minimums from your own experience and comfort level, tighter than the regulations, with adjustments for pilot factors such as fatigue, stress, and emotion. Writing them before the pressure arrives is the whole point.`},
        {t:`Copy Abby's endorsement limits straight onto the card as her minimums.`, s:0, flag:`nopersonalmins`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Abby reads it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"These are my numbers."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"They're good numbers."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"They're the ceiling I'll let you have. Your card is the ceiling you give yourself. Could end up the same. You have to get there on your own, though."</span> Jordan says she'll think about it. Then she copies Abby's numbers onto the card in her own writing anyway, and from across the table it almost passes for hers.`,
          why:`Instructor limitations are a legal ceiling under 61.89(a)(8), not a personal assessment. Personal minimums come from the pilot's own experience, comfort, and condition, including the pilot factors that no endorsement mentions.`},
        {t:`Write weather and wind numbers tighter than Abby's, but skip the pilot lines.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Better numbers than mine. I like that."</span> She turns the card over and back. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Where are you on it? IMSAFE is a question. A card is the answer you wrote before you were tired."</span> Jordan thinks about Wednesday, and adds a line at the bottom in smaller letters, and then another one.`,
          why:`Tighter weather and wind numbers are good. But the RMH adjusts for pilot factors like illness, stress, and fatigue, and IMSAFE answered fresh each morning is exactly what fatigue and emotion distort. Write the pilot limits down too.`},
        {t:`Leave it blank and decide fresh each day with IMSAFE and PAVE.`, s:0, flag:`nopersonalmins`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"No card, no practice area."</span> She doesn't raise her voice. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Deciding fresh every day is how you end up deciding in the parking lot. The handbook says don't set your minimums to fit the flight in front of you."</span> That night, so she'll get the signature, Jordan copies Abby's limits onto the card, word for word.`,
          why:`The RMH's first caution is not to adjust personal minimums to complete a specific flight. Deciding fresh each time invites exactly that. Written minimums set ahead of time make the no-go call easier, especially under pressure.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s12`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Nine o'clock`, q:`Ellie made a sign to watch Jordan land this morning. How should Jordan schedule the solo?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, November 7, 6:05 in the morning, the kitchen table, the card propped against the sugar bowl. Solo at 7:30, three laps, the slot she'd booked on Tuesday. Claire had texted last night that she'd bring Ellie by at nine to watch, on the way to the 10:30 game, the last one of the season. <i>She wants to see you land. She made a sign.</i>`,`<prop type="doc" title="KAPA, Saturday 5:53 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 071253Z 34005KT 10SM SKC M02/M09 A3021 RMK AO2 SLP251 T10171089<br><br>TAF KAPA 071120Z 0712/0812 34006KT P6SM SKC<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM071600 32014G22KT P6SM FEW100<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM080000 33008KT P6SM SKC</code></prop>`,`She did the conversion out loud this time, slowly, like a dose. Sixteen hundred Zulu. Winter. Minus seven. Nine o'clock. Fourteen gusting twenty two, over every number on the card. The wind was coming the same hour Ellie was.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fly at 7:30 as booked and have Claire bring Ellie at 8.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She texts Claire at 6:10. <b>Wind's due at 9. I'll be done by 8:30. Come at 8 for a landing, or come at 9 and we'll sit in her.</b> Claire: <b>We'll come at 8. She's already in her cleats.</b> Runup on Alpha, ready for departure, monitor tower, and wheels up at 7:34. Two laps, smooth as glass. On the third downwind, at 8:04, the windsock by the tower lifts off its pole and swings, and the tower gives a Cirrus the wind, three two zero at nine, gusts one five. An hour early. One knot of gust over Abby's limit. She flies the last approach a little fast with a little more left wing down than she'd like, and puts it on, and calls it a full stop. At the fence, a nine year old holds up a poster board that says NICE LANDING in purple, which she'd made the night before, on faith.`,
          why:`The PHAK says to manage external pressure by managing passengers' expectations and leaving yourself an out. A forecast can be wrong in either direction, and here the wind came early. Flying in the calm window with a buffer, and moving the audience instead of the flight, kept her inside the card.`},
        {t:`Fly at nine, for Ellie. It's calm now, and TAFs can be wrong.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`At 8:04 the windsock lifts and swings. By 8:30 the ATIS is three two zero at twelve, gusts two zero. When Claire's car pulls in at nine, Jordan is standing at the tie down with the card in her hand and the airplane tied down, and she has to tell Ellie no again, which she's getting worse at, not better. She thinks about how it would have gone if the wind had come at nine like it said, with her on short final.`,
          why:`Delaying into a forecast wind change to satisfy a spectator is external pressure making the decision. The PHAK reminds pilots the weather may differ from the forecast, and it did: the wind arrived early.`},
        {t:`Take off at 8:30 so Ellie catches the last landing at nine.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`At 8:04, during her preflight, the windsock lifts. By 8:20 the ATIS has changed, and it's over the card, and she puts the tie down chains back on. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"A plan with no buffer is a plan that needs the forecast to be perfect,"</span> Abby says later, on the phone. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"They never are. At least you looked."</span>`,
          why:`Timing the last landing for the minute the wind is forecast leaves no margin for a forecast that's early, which happens. Planning a buffer is part of managing external pressure.`},
        {t:`Cancel the solo, and show Ellie the airplane on the ground.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:2},
          after:`She cancels at 6:15 and goes back to bed, and when the wind comes early at 8:04 she feels very smart for about an hour. Abby, when she hears: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Nothing wrong with no. But you had two calm hours and an out. Accept no unnecessary risk works both ways. When the benefit's real and the risk's small, you can take it."</span>`,
          why:`Canceling is always a safe choice. But the risk management principles also say to accept risk when the benefits outweigh the costs. A calm two hour window with a clear stop time and margin was a reasonable risk to accept.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f8s3`, title:`Bottle to throttle, with the clocks going back`,
     setup:[`Suppose she'd said yes to three beers, the last one finished at 11:20 PM on Saturday, October 31, Mountain Daylight Time, UTC minus 6. The clocks fall back at 2:00 AM Sunday to Mountain Standard Time, UTC minus 7. Her solo is 7:00 AM Sunday, MST.`,`91.17: no flying within 8 hours of any alcohol. The PHAK: the body needs about 3 hours to clear one beer. Abby counts from the last drink, which is conservative on purpose. The AIM: an excellent rule is 12 to 24 hours.`,`Enter clock times as numbers without the colon, so 7:00 AM is 700 and 2:00 PM is 1400.`],
     fields:[{label:`Last beer in UTC (Nov 1)`,answer:520,tol:0},{label:`Solo time in UTC`,answer:1400,tol:0},{label:`Real hours between them`,answer:8.67,tol:0.02},{label:`Hours the kitchen clock would show, ignoring the time change`,answer:7.67,tol:0.02},{label:`Earliest legal time under the 8 hour rule (MST)`,answer:620,tol:0},{label:`PHAK hours to clear three beers`,answer:9,tol:0},{label:`Time she'd be clear by that count (MST)`,answer:720,tol:0},{label:`Earliest by the AIM's 12 hour lower bound (MST)`,answer:1020,tol:0}],
     solution:[`11:20 PM MDT is 23:20. Add 6 hours to get UTC: 29:20, which is 05:20 UTC on November 1.`,`7:00 AM MST plus 7 hours is 14:00 UTC.`,`14:00 minus 05:20 is 8 hours 40 minutes, or 8.67 hours. The kitchen clock says 11:20 PM to 7:00 AM, which is 7 hours 40 minutes, 7.67 hours. The time change gave her an hour she didn't feel.`,`8 hour rule: 05:20 + 8:00 = 13:20 UTC. Minus 7 hours is 6:20 AM MST. Legal by 40 minutes, if her alcohol concentration is under 0.04 and she isn't impaired, and a hangover counts as impaired.`,`PHAK: 3 beers × 3 hours = 9 hours. 05:20 + 9:00 = 14:20 UTC = 7:20 AM MST. Twenty minutes after the solo.`,`AIM: 05:20 + 12:00 = 17:20 UTC = 10:20 AM MST, at the very least.`,`Legal and clear are different clocks. The regulation only tells you the earliest you're not violating it.`]},
    {after:`f8s4`, title:`Russ, the Divide, and oxygen`,
     setup:[`Sunday's return. Russ's plan from the nav log he posted: climbing through 12,500 at 9:25, level at 13,500, starting down through 12,500 at 10:10.`,`91.211(a)(1): above 12,500 up to and including 14,000, the crew uses oxygen for the part of the flight at those altitudes over 30 minutes. (a)(2): above 14,000, the entire time at those altitudes.`,`The PHAK: one ounce of alcohol can equal about 2,000 feet of physiological altitude. The AIM: night vision deteriorates at cabin altitudes as low as 5,000 feet. KAPA is at 5,885 feet.`,`Enter clock times as numbers without the colon, so 9:25 is 925.`],
     fields:[{label:`Minutes above 12,500`,answer:45,tol:0},{label:`Minutes the crew must be on oxygen`,answer:15,tol:0},{label:`Clock time oxygen becomes required`,answer:955,tol:0},{label:`If 25 minutes of the leg were above 14,000 in an airplane that can get there, minutes of oxygen required`,answer:25,tol:0},{label:`Physiological altitude at 13,500 after one ounce of alcohol (feet)`,answer:15500,tol:0},{label:`How far KAPA sits above the AIM's 5,000 foot night vision line (feet)`,answer:885,tol:0}],
     solution:[`9:25 to 10:10 is 45 minutes between 12,500 and 14,000.`,`The rule covers the part over 30 minutes: 45 minus 30 = 15 minutes. 9:25 + 30 minutes = 9:55, so the cannula goes on by then. Marisol would tell you to put it on at the start, and she'd be right.`,`Above 14,000 there's no free 30 minutes. 25 minutes above 14,000 means 25 minutes on oxygen, all of it.`,`13,500 + 2,000 = 15,500 feet of physiological altitude. Above 15,000, the regulation starts talking about oxygen for everybody on board. Alcohol takes you there without the airplane going anywhere. Your body is there. The regulation still counts the altimeter.`,`5,885 minus 5,000 = 885 feet. Every night flight from Centennial starts above the altitude where the AIM says night vision begins to suffer.`]},
    {after:`f8s11`, title:`Adjusting the baseline`,
     setup:[`The baseline column of Jordan's worksheet, solo in the KAPA pattern: ceiling 3,500 feet AGL, visibility 6 statute miles, surface wind 10 knots, shortest runway 4,800 feet.`,`RMH Figure 2-9 starting points, per factor that applies: add at least 500 feet to ceiling, at least 1/2 mile to visibility, at least 500 feet to runway length, and subtract at least 5 knots from winds.`,`Factors on a given morning might be emotion (a hard date) and fatigue (the morning after a shift). KAPA field elevation 5,885 feet. Saturday's 1600Z forecast wind: 14 gusting 22.`],
     fields:[{label:`Ceiling with one factor (feet AGL)`,answer:4000,tol:0},{label:`Visibility with one factor (SM)`,answer:6.5,tol:0},{label:`Wind with one factor (knots)`,answer:5,tol:0},{label:`Runway with one factor (feet)`,answer:5300,tol:0},{label:`Ceiling with two factors (feet AGL)`,answer:4500,tol:0},{label:`Wind with two factors (knots)`,answer:0,tol:0},{label:`That two factor ceiling in feet MSL over KAPA`,answer:10385,tol:0},{label:`Gust factor in Saturday's forecast (knots)`,answer:8,tol:0}],
     solution:[`One factor: 3,500 + 500 = 4,000 ft AGL. 6 + 0.5 = 6.5 SM. 10 minus 5 = 5 knots. 4,800 + 500 = 5,300 ft of runway.`,`Two factors: 3,500 + 1,000 = 4,500 ft AGL, 7 SM, 10 minus 10 = 0 knots, and 5,800 ft of runway. Zero knots of wind is the handbook's polite way of saying stay home, which is why her card says two factors, go home.`,`4,500 AGL + 5,885 = 10,385 MSL. Ceilings in a METAR are AGL. The Class B floor is MSL. Keep them straight.`,`Saturday: 22 minus 14 = 8 knots of gust factor, and 14 knots of steady wind. Over her baseline before any adjustment, and over Abby's limit of 10 with gusts no more than 5 over.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f8s10`, title:`Attitudes and antidotes`, intro:`After the meeting, Walt left the whiteboard up. Jordan copied it into the back of her logbook and quizzed herself in the parking lot. Match each one.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`The antidote for anti-authority.`, choices:[`It could happen to me.`,`Follow the rules. They are usually right.`,`Not so fast. Think first.`], answer:1, why:`PHAK Figure 2-4. Anti-authority, "Don't tell me," is answered with "Follow the rules. They are usually right." You can still question a rule you think is wrong.`},
      {prompt:`The antidote for impulsivity.`, choices:[`Not so fast. Think first.`,`Taking chances is foolish.`,`I'm not helpless. I can make a difference.`], answer:0, why:`Impulsivity, "Do it quickly," is answered with "Not so fast. Think first."`},
      {prompt:`The antidote for invulnerability.`, choices:[`Follow the rules. They are usually right.`,`Taking chances is foolish.`,`It could happen to me.`], answer:2, why:`Invulnerability, "It won't happen to me," is answered with "It could happen to me."`},
      {prompt:`The antidote for macho.`, choices:[`Taking chances is foolish.`,`Not so fast. Think first.`,`It could happen to me.`], answer:0, why:`Macho, "I can do it," is answered with "Taking chances is foolish." The PHAK notes women are equally susceptible.`},
      {prompt:`The antidote for resignation.`, choices:[`Follow the rules. They are usually right.`,`I'm not helpless. I can make a difference.`,`Not so fast. Think first.`], answer:1, why:`Resignation, "What's the use?", is answered with "I'm not helpless. I can make a difference."`},
      {prompt:`"Forecasters are always wrong anyway. Nobody grounds me."`, choices:[`Resignation`,`Anti-authority`,`Impulsivity`], answer:1, why:`Treating the information and the people who provide it as something to resent and ignore is anti-authority.`},
      {prompt:`"Watch this."`, choices:[`Macho`,`Invulnerability`,`Resignation`], answer:0, why:`Taking a risk to impress somebody is macho, "I can do it." Its antidote is "Taking chances is foolish."`},
      {prompt:`"Tower will tell me if something's wrong. Whatever happens, happens."`, choices:[`Impulsivity`,`Anti-authority`,`Resignation`], answer:2, why:`Leaving the action to others because you don't think you can make a difference is resignation.`},
      {prompt:`The engine coughs. "Turn back. No, land on that road. No, the field."`, choices:[`Impulsivity`,`Macho`,`Invulnerability`], answer:0, why:`Doing the first thing that comes to mind, then the next, without stopping to choose the best alternative, is impulsivity.`},
      {prompt:`"Fuel's legal. Other people run tanks dry, not me."`, choices:[`Anti-authority`,`Invulnerability`,`Macho`], answer:1, why:`Knowing accidents happen but believing they happen to other people is invulnerability.`},
      {prompt:`"Stopping now means I failed."`, choices:[`Resignation`,`Impulsivity`,`Macho`], answer:2, why:`Pressing on to prove you can is macho, "I can do it." Its antidote is "Taking chances is foolish."`},
      {prompt:`The first step to neutralizing a hazardous thought, per the PHAK.`, choices:[`Recognize it and label it hazardous`,`Ask ATC for advice`,`Wait to see if it passes`], answer:0, why:`Recognition is the first step. Label the thought as hazardous, then state the corresponding antidote, which is why the antidotes are memorized.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`The antidote "Follow the rules. They are usually right." is meant to counter which hazardous attitude?`, opts:[`resignation`,`anti-authority`,`invulnerability`], a:1, why:`PHAK Figure 2-4: anti-authority, "Don't tell me," is countered by "Follow the rules. They are usually right." Resignation's antidote is "I'm not helpless. I can make a difference." Invulnerability's is "It could happen to me."`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A pilot with a mild cold is most likely to experience a painful ear block`, opts:[`during climb`,`in level cruise`,`during descent`], a:2, why:`On descent the outside pressure rises and the middle ear has to be actively equalized through the Eustachian tube, which congestion can block. The AIM says not to fly with an upper respiratory infection or nasal allergy.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Which antidote does the PHAK give for the impulsivity attitude?`, opts:[`It could happen to me.`,`Taking chances is foolish.`,`Not so fast. Think first.`], a:2, why:`Impulsivity, "Do it quickly," is countered by "Not so fast. Think first." The other two are the antidotes for invulnerability and macho.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`14 CFR 91.17 prohibits acting as a crewmember of a civil aircraft within how long after consuming any alcoholic beverage?`, opts:[`8 hours`,`12 hours`,`24 hours`], a:0, why:`91.17(a)(1): within 8 hours. The AIM recommends 12 to 24 hours as a better practice, depending on the amount, but the regulation is 8.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Under 91.17, no person may act as a crewmember while having an alcohol concentration in a blood or breath specimen of`, opts:[`0.08 or greater`,`0.04 or greater`,`0.02 or greater`], a:1, why:`91.17(a)(4): 0.04 or greater. The 8 hour rule, the under the influence rule, and the 0.04 rule all apply separately.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Under 91.211, at cabin pressure altitudes above 12,500 feet MSL up to and including 14,000 feet MSL, the required flight crew must use supplemental oxygen`, opts:[`for the entire time at those altitudes`,`for any part over 30 minutes there`,`only when the flight is made at night`], a:1, why:`91.211(a)(1): for that part of the flight at those altitudes that is of more than 30 minutes duration. Above 14,000 the crew uses it the entire time.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Under 91.211, at cabin pressure altitudes above 15,000 feet MSL, each occupant of the aircraft must be`, opts:[`provided with supplemental oxygen`,`using supplemental oxygen throughout`,`briefed on the oxygen equipment`], a:0, why:`91.211(a)(3): each occupant must be provided with supplemental oxygen. Passengers aren't required to use it. The crew must use it above 14,000.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Carbon monoxide poisoning from a leaking cabin heater causes which type of hypoxia?`, opts:[`histotoxic hypoxia`,`stagnant hypoxia`,`hypemic hypoxia`], a:2, why:`PHAK: CO binds hemoglobin so the blood can't carry oxygen, hypemic hypoxia, its most common form. Histotoxic is cells unable to use oxygen, as with alcohol. Stagnant is blood not flowing.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A pilot feels lightheaded and tingly after a scare in flight and is breathing rapidly. The best treatment is`, opts:[`breathing faster to raise oxygen levels`,`slowing the breathing rate or talking aloud`,`climbing to a higher, cooler altitude`], a:1, why:`That's hyperventilation. The PHAK says slowing the breathing rate, breathing into a paper bag, or talking aloud restores the carbon dioxide level, and recovery is usually rapid.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An abrupt head movement during a prolonged constant-rate turn in instrument conditions can produce`, opts:[`the leans`,`inversion illusion`,`Coriolis illusion`], a:2, why:`AIM 8-1-5: the Coriolis illusion, the most overwhelming of all illusions in flight. The leans come from leveling after an unnoticed bank. Inversion comes from an abrupt change from climb to level.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A runway narrower than the pilot is used to can create the illusion of being higher than actual, which tends to cause`, opts:[`a lower than normal approach`,`a higher than normal approach`,`a flare well above the runway`], a:0, why:`PHAK and AIM: a narrower than usual runway makes the airplane seem higher, so the pilot flies a lower approach and risks striking objects or landing short.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`According to the Risk Management Handbook, a pilot should consider adjusting personal minimums`, opts:[`to fit the flight being planned`,`whenever the weather improves`,`when time permits honest review`], a:2, why:`RMH chapter 2: don't adjust personal minimums to complete a specific flight. Adjust them when time and objectivity permit an honest self-analysis, one variable at a time, ideally with a flight instructor.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, weather. In November at KAPA, after the change to standard time, a TAF line beginning FM071600 takes effect at`, opts:[`9:00 AM local`,`10:00 AM local`,`4:00 PM local`], a:0, why:`Mountain Standard Time is UTC minus 7, so 1600Z is 0900 local. In summer, on daylight time, it's minus 6, and the same line would start at 10 AM.`, task:`I.C`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Under the hood, the attitude indicator shows the nose low and a right bank, and the airspeed is climbing fast. The correct recovery order is`, opts:[`raise the nose, level the wings, then reduce power`,`reduce power, level the wings, then raise the nose`,`level the wings, raise the nose, then add power`], a:1, why:`Instrument Flying Handbook, nose-low unusual attitude: reduce power first so the airspeed stops building, level the wings with coordinated aileron and rudder, then raise the nose with smooth back pressure. Pulling while still banked mostly tightens the turn and loads the airplane, which is how a graveyard spiral starts.`, task:`VIII`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, emergencies. The engine quits at 7,500 feet over the practice area. The pilot's first action should be to`, opts:[`pitch for best glide speed`,`turn back toward the runway`,`try an engine restart first`], a:0, why:`Fly the airplane first: establish the best glide speed to get the most distance from the altitude you have. Then pick a landing area, then troubleshoot and attempt a restart.`, task:`IX`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`After a dive that did not require a controlled ascent, the AIM recommends waiting at least how long before flying to an altitude of up to 8,000 feet MSL?`, opts:[`12 hours`,`24 hours`,`4 hours`], a:0, why:`AIM 8-1-2: wait at least 12 hours after diving that didn't require a controlled ascent before flying up to 8,000 feet MSL, and at least 24 hours after diving that did. Above 8,000 feet, wait at least 24 hours after any dive.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A pilot took an over the counter antihistamine for allergies this morning. What should the pilot do about a flight this afternoon?`, opts:[`Fly, but stay below 5,000 feet where the effects are weaker`,`Not fly until the side effects and the allergy are gone`,`Fly, and take a second dose in case symptoms come back`], a:1, why:`PHAK ch 17 and the AIM: many over the counter medicines, antihistamines among them, cause drowsiness and slow reactions. IMSAFE's M asks about anything that affects judgment. The safe answer is not to fly while the medicine or the condition can impair you.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`According to the PHAK, the first noticeable effect of dehydration is`, opts:[`thirst`,`dizziness`,`fatigue`], a:2, why:`PHAK ch 17: fatigue is the first noticeable effect of dehydration. Thirst comes late, after about a quart and a half of water is already lost.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A student pilot becomes airsick during a lesson. Which action is most likely to help?`, opts:[`Open the fresh air vents and look outside at the horizon`,`Take an antihistamine such as Dramamine in flight`,`Close the vents and look down at the instrument panel`], a:0, why:`PHAK ch 17: open the air vents, keep your eyes on a point outside, and avoid unnecessary head movement. Motion sickness drugs aren't recommended for pilots because they can make you drowsy.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`In straight and level flight under the hood, which instrument is primary for pitch?`, opts:[`The attitude indicator`,`The altimeter`,`The airspeed indicator`], a:1, why:`Instrument Flying Handbook: in straight and level flight the altimeter is primary for pitch, the heading indicator for bank, and the airspeed indicator for power. The attitude indicator supports pitch and bank; the tachometer supports power.`, task:`VIII`, domain:`Airport and flight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `She'd read the next page of the notebook on Friday night, after the card, sitting on the floor of her apartment with her back against the couch instead of the dishwasher, which she counted as progress.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from February, eight years ago">Feb 3. KAPA, pattern, 0.7. El has had an ear infection since Tuesday and has screamed since Tuesday, and I have slept maybe 9 hours since then, total. Came out to clear my head. My head felt clear the whole time, I want that on the record. Landing 1: fine. Landing 2: flared at like twenty feet and she dropped like a piano. Landing 3: I remember the takeoff and I remember the flare and I do not remember the downwind. At all. Taxied in, sat in the car to warm up, woke up two hours later with Marisol knocking on the window holding a coffee. Claire had called four times. Lesson: tired doesn't feel like tired. It feels like fine. NEW RULE. Taped El's picture above the mag switch. If I can't look at her and say out loud that I slept, I go home. (Marisol says a picture isn't a rule. Marisol can mind her own business.)</prop>`,
    `She'd laughed at the last line, one bark, and then she'd stopped, because she knew where the picture was now. Third grade, a green sweater, two corners of yellow tape, under a pizza magnet on Claire's fridge.`,
    ...((s.picks||{})[6]===1?[`Her own logbook was on the floor beside her, open to Wednesday, where Abby had written in green: <b>LESSON TERMINATED BY INSTRUCTOR.</b>`]:(s.picks||{})[6]===2?[`Her own logbook was on the floor beside her, open to Wednesday, where Abby had written in green: <b>LESSON TERMINATED, FULL STOP AFTER ONE LANDING. SEE ME.</b>`]:[]),
    `${(s.picks||{})[11]===0?`Saturday, by 8:45, Biscuit was tied down with the chains tight and the wind coming hard out of the northwest, an hour ahead of the TAF. The NICE LANDING sign was propped against the left main tire.`:`Saturday, by nine, the wind had come in hard out of the northwest, an hour before the TAF had said it would, and Biscuit sat at the tie down with her chains tight. Claire's car pulled in at nine on the dot.`} Ellie climbed into the right seat in her shin guards, which was allowed, because Biscuit wasn't going anywhere, and Claire stood at the open door with her arms folded against the cold.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, plain, holding it together.">"He kept her picture there,"</span> Claire said, before Jordan could say anything, looking at the dark rectangle above the mag switch. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, plain, holding it together.">"Every September he put in the new school one. I took it down the week after. I couldn't stand it out here with nobody looking at it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I know. He wrote it down."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Of course he did."</span>`,
    `Jordan got the white tape out of her jump bag, the inch-wide kind she used to hold down IV lines, and tore four strips with her teeth. She held the card over the rectangle for a second to see how it fit. It fit. Then she pressed down the corners, one at a time, right over the two yellowed ones he'd left.`,
    ((s.flags.nopersonalmins||G.nopersonalmins)
      ?`<prop type="doc" title="Index card, taped above the mag switch">STUDENT SOLO LIMITS (A. STRAND)<br>DAY VFR. KAPA PATTERN ONLY.<br>WIND 10 OR LESS. GUSTS NO MORE THAN 5 OVER. CROSSWIND 5.<br>CEILING 3,000 AGL. VISIBILITY 5 SM.<br>NO PASSENGERS. LATCH BOTH DOORS.</prop>`
      :`<prop type="doc" title="Index card, taped above the mag switch">J. REYES. PERSONAL MINIMUMS. MINE, NOT THE FAA'S, NOT ABBY'S.<br>SOLO, KAPA PATTERN: CEILING 3,500 AGL. VIS 6 SM. WIND 10, GUSTS 5 OVER, CROSSWIND 5. IF IT'S CLOSE, IT'S NO.<br>7 HOURS OF SLEEP OR I DON'T GO. NO SOLO THE MORNING AFTER A SHIFT.<br>NOTHING TO DRINK FOR 12 HOURS. SICK OR TAKING ANYTHING, I DON'T GO.<br>EMOTION IS WEATHER. MARCH AND NOV 5: DUAL OR NOTHING.<br>ONE FACTOR: ADD 500 FT, 1/2 MILE, TAKE 5 KNOTS OFF. TWO FACTORS: GO HOME.<br>I DON'T CHANGE THIS CARD IN THE PARKING LOT.</prop>`),
    `Ellie leaned over from the right seat to read it, mouthing the words. She knew where the picture used to be. You could tell from her face that she knew.`,
    ((s.flags.nopersonalmins||G.nopersonalmins)
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"That's all numbers."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"They're Abby's numbers."</span> Ellie read it again, frowning, the way she read a math problem with a trick in it. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Where are yours?"</span> Jordan didn't have an answer that fit in the cockpit, so she said she was working on it, which Ellie accepted the way she accepted most things grownups said, provisionally.`
      :`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Emotion is weather,"</span> Ellie read, sounding it out the way she did spelling words at the kitchen table. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"That's a kind of weather?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Mine, yeah."</span> Ellie chewed the inside of her cheek. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie" data-tone="Quiet, serious, searching.">"Daddy had my picture there."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I know, El."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ellie" data-tone="Quiet, serious, searching.">"Is a card better than a picture?"</span> There was no faking it with her. Jordan thought about it for real. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Gentle, honest, steady.">"No. This one's mine. The picture was his thing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Okay."</span> Then, frowning at the card, like a teacher: <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"You spelled everything right."</span>`),
    `Under the card the HI DAD sticker had a corner up. Jordan pressed it flat with her thumb. Up it came again. Fine.`,
    `Claire stayed at the door with her arms folded for a while. Eventually she reached in past Jordan and pressed two fingers on the card, fast, the way you check a pulse on your way out of a room. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"He'd hate it. Not one joke on there."</span> She took the hand back. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"It's good."</span> A look at her watch. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Ellie. Cleats. We're late, and I don't do late."</span>`,
    `Ellie went over the seat back like a cat and paused on the step. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Are you coming to my game?"</span>`,
    `It was the last one of the season. Ten thirty, the fields by the rec center. Jordan was off. She'd checked on Tuesday, without telling herself why.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Yeah, El. I'm off."</span>`,
    `Ellie sprinted for the car, braids blown flat sideways. Claire walked. Jordan stayed in the left seat another minute, hands on her knees, and read the card top to bottom once more, same as a run report before she signs it. Every line.`,
    `Her phone buzzed against her leg.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="9:41 AM">${(s.score>=27)?`Practice area endorsement Tuesday after one dual. Then cross-country ground school. Buy a plotter. Not the fancy one. Bring the card, I want to see where you put it.`:`Practice area endorsement Tuesday after one dual, if Tuesday goes how I want it to. Then cross-country ground school. Buy a plotter. Not the fancy one.`}</prop>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch08.mp3`, len:`about 60 minutes`},
  next:`Cross-Country`
});
